hadiths[429]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6601","7022 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from an elder of Ashja', he said: We were with Salim, then he mentioned the same."
"6602","7023 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, then he mentioned with his chain the same. Abu Ja'far said: Some held this view, saying: thus the sneezer should say and be told, as in this hadith; this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. Others disagreed, saying: rather the sneezer says after being blessed: May Allah guide you and set your minds right."
"6603","7024 - And they argued with what Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, that he heard Ubayd ibn Umm Kilab say: I heard Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Talib say: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sneezed, he praised Allah, and it was said to him: May Allah have mercy on you, and he would say to them: May Allah guide you and set your minds right."
"6604","7025 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Najih, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she said: A man sneezed before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: What should I say, O Prophet of Allah? He said: Say: Praise be to Allah. The people said: What should we say to him, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Say: May Allah have mercy on you. He said: What should I say to them? He said: Say: May Allah guide you and set your minds right. The first group said: the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: May Allah guide you and set your minds right, was because those present were Jews, and his teaching the sneezer in A'ishah's (may Allah be pleased with her) hadith to say: May Allah guide you and set your minds right, was because those present then were Jews."
"6605","7026 - And they argued with what Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hakim ibn al-Daylam, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, he said: The Jews used to sneeze before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hoping he would say: May Allah have mercy on you, but he would say: May Allah guide you and set your minds right."
"6606","7027 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hakim ibn al-Daylam, from al-Dahhak, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. They said: the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: May Allah guide you and set your minds right, to the Jews was as in this hadith. As for Muslims, they say as in Salim ibn Ubayd's hadith mentioned at the start. They have no proof in this hadith against the other view, for this hadith only says the Jews sneezed before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hoping he would say: May Allah have mercy on you, and he would say: May Allah guide you and set your minds right. This saying was to the Jews though sneezing. They and their opponents do not differ over what the blesser says to the sneezer; their difference is over what the sneezer says after being blessed, and Abu Musa's hadith contains nothing of that, so it does not contradict Abd Allah ibn Ja'far's hadith nor A'ishah's (may Allah be pleased with her) hadith."
"6607","7028 - And they argued with what is narrated from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us. 7029 - And Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Wasil, from Ibrahim, he said: May Allah guide you and set your minds right - is said at sneezing; the Khawarij said it because they did not seek forgiveness for people. Such is Abu Bishr's wording; Muhammad ibn Amr's hadith lacks: because they did not seek forgiveness for people. It was said to them: how could the Khawarij have innovated this when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say it and teach it to his companions?"
"6608","7030 - And there is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from his brother, from his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah; and let his brother or companion say to him: May Allah have mercy on you; and let him say: May Allah guide you and set your minds right. Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6609","7031 - Rabi' the muezzin and Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Thus what Ibrahim said is refuted, and what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more authentic and evident than what opposes it, so it is dearer to us than what contradicts it."
"6610","7032 - Chapter: A man with a disease, is he avoided or not? Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Salamah said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not bring the sick to the healthy. Al-Harith ibn Abi Dhubab said to him: You had narrated to us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion, but Abu Hurayrah denied that. Al-Harith said: Yes indeed. He and Abu Hurayrah disputed until matters intensified; Abu Hurayrah grew angry and said to al-Harith - Muslim mentioned it - then spoke in Abyssinian, then said to al-Harith: Do you know what I said? Al-Harith said: No. I said: you mean by that that I did not narrate to you what you say. Abu Salamah said: I do not know whether Abu Hurayrah forgot or resembled, but I never saw him forget a word he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except his denial of what he used to narrate in his saying: There is no contagion."
"6611","7033 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Abu Salamah narrated to him that Abu Hurayrah narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No sick one is brought to a healthy one. Abu Salamah said: Abu Hurayrah used to narrate both from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Abu Hurayrah fell silent afterward about: There is no contagion, and maintained: No sick one is brought to a healthy one, then he narrated like Ibn Abi Dawud's hadith. Abu Ja'far said: Some held this, disliking bringing the sick to the healthy, saying it was disliked for fear of contagion, and they were commanded to avoid the diseased and flee from him. They also argued with what is narrated from Umar about the plague, his returning with the people fleeing from it."
"6612","7034 - They mentioned what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) set out for Syria, and Abu Talhah and Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah met him, saying: Commander of the Believers, with you are the eminent companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their best, and behind us we left like a blazing fire; return this year - meaning: so Umar returned. The next year he came and entered - meaning: the plague."
"6613","7035 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab went out to Syria until when at Sargh, the commanders of the armies, Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah and his companions, met him and told him the epidemic had struck Syria. Ibn Abbas said: Umar said: Call for me the first Muhajirun. They called them and he consulted them, telling them the epidemic had struck Syria, and they differed. Some said: You set out for a purpose and we see no returning from it. Others said: With you are the rest of the people and the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we see no exposing them to this epidemic. He said: Leave me. Then he said: Call for me the Ansar. They called them, and they followed the Muhajirun's way, differing likewise. He said: Leave me. Then he said: Call for me the elders of Quraysh among the Muhajirun of the Conquest present here. They called them, and no two of them differed: they said: we see you return with the people and not expose them to this epidemic. So Umar announced among the people to mount by morning, and they mounted. Abu Ubaydah said: Fleeing from Allah's decree? Umar said: Would that another than you said it, O Abu Ubaydah; yes, we flee from Allah's decree to Allah's decree. What if you had camels descending a valley with two sides, one fertile and one barren - if you grazed the fertile, would you not graze it by Allah's decree, and if you grazed the barren, by Allah's decree? He said: Then Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came, having been absent for a need, and said: I have knowledge of this; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear of it in a land, do not proceed to it, and when it strikes a land while you are in it, do not leave fleeing from it. So Umar praised Allah and departed."
"6614","7036 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Malik, he narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab went out to Syria. When he reached Sargh, he learned the epidemic had struck Syria, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioning what is in Yunus's preceding hadith, specifically Abd al-Rahman's hadith. He said: So Umar returned from Sargh."
"6615","7037 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, when intending to return from Sargh, consulted the people. A group, among them Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, said: Do you flee from death? We are but by decree, and nothing befalls us except what Allah wrote for us. Umar said: O Abu Ubaydah, had you been in a valley, one side fertile and the other barren, which would you graze? He said: The fertile. He said: Whether we advance or retreat, it is by decree, and in decree we are."
"6616","7038 - al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us. 7039 - And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, he said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab say: We used to converse with Abu Musa al-Ash'ari. One day he said to us: You need not conceal from me, for this plague has struck my family; whoever wishes to withdraw, let him withdraw. Beware of two things: that one says: one who left was safe and one who stayed was struck - had I left I would have been safe like so-and-so's family; or one says: had I stayed I would have been struck like so-and-so's family. I shall tell you what people should do in plague: I was with Abu Ubaydah when plague struck Syria, and Umar wrote to him: when this letter reaches you, I adjure you - if it reaches you by morning, do not evening before riding to me; if by evening, do not morning before riding to me, for a need concerning you has arisen for which I need you. When Abu Ubaydah read the letter, he said: The Commander of the Believers wishes to preserve one who will not survive. So Abu Ubaydah wrote to him: I am among an army of Muslims; I fled from death and travel will not make me prefer myself over them; we know the Commander's need, so release me from your adjuration. When the letter reached Umar, he wept. It was said to him: Has Abu Ubaydah died? He said: No. Umar had written to him: al-Urdunn is low land, and al-Jabiyah is wholesome land, so move with the Muslims to al-Jabiyah. Abu Ubaydah said to me: Go and settle the Muslims in their place. I said: I cannot. He said: So he went to mount, and a man among the people said to me - he said: he was seized, afflicted, died, and the plague lifted. They said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) thus ordered people to leave the plague, agreed by the Prophet's companions, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what agrees. And others besides Abd al-Rahman narrated likewise from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6617","7040 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Hadrami, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When plague is in a land and you are in it, do not flee from it; and when it is in a land, do not descend upon it."
"6618","7041 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Hadrami narrated to us that Lahiq narrated to him that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrated to him, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6619","7042 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: This pain and sickness is a punishment with which some nations before you were punished; then it remained on earth, departing at times and coming at others. Whoever hears of it in a land, let him not proceed to it; and whoever is in a land where it strikes, let not fleeing expel him from it."
"6620","7043 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, he said: I heard Usamah ibn Zayd narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: This plague is a punishment and torment with which a people were punished; when it is in a land, do not descend upon it, and when it strikes while you are in a land, do not leave it."
"6621","7044 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me, from Abu al-Nadr, from Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, that he heard his father ask Usamah ibn Zayd: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention the plague? He said: Yes. He said: How did you hear him? He said: I heard him say: It is a punishment Allah imposed upon the Children of Israel, or upon a people; when you hear of it in a land, do not proceed to it, and if it strikes while you are in a land, do not leave fleeing from it."
"6622","7045 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn al-Munkadir and Abu al-Nadr, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6623","7046 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the plague was mentioned before him and he said: It is a filth - or: punishment - with which a nation was punished, and remnants of it remain. Then he mentioned like Yunus's hadith, adding: Muhammad said to me: I narrated this hadith to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he said to me: Thus Amir ibn Sa'd narrated to me."
"6624","7047 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi narrated to us, from his father - or: from his uncle - from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the expedition of Tabuk: When plague strikes a land and you are in it, do not leave it; and when you are elsewhere, do not proceed to it."
"6625","7048 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Humayd, he said: I heard Shurahbil ibn Hasanah narrate from Amr ibn al-As: The plague struck Syria, so Amr said: Disperse from it, for it is a punishment. That reached Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, and he said: I accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him say: It is your Lord's mercy, your Prophet's supplication, and the death of the righteous before you; so gather for it and do not disperse from it. Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: He spoke truth. They said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports commanded not to proceed to the plague, out of fear of it. It was said to them: there is no proof in this, for had he commanded avoiding proceeding out of fear, he would have permitted the people of the stricken place to leave it, since fear for them equals fear for others. Since he forbade the people of the plague-stricken place from leaving it, the reason for forbidding proceeding is other than what you claim. If one asks: what is that reason? It was said: in our view - and Allah knows best - lest a man proceed to it and be afflicted by Allah's decree, then say: had I not come to this land, this ailment would not have struck me - though had he stayed where he left, it might have struck him; so he was commanded not to proceed, fearing this saying. Likewise he was commanded not to leave the land he is in, lest he be safe and say: had I stayed in that land, what struck its people would have struck me - though had he stayed, nothing might have struck him. So he was commanded to avoid proceeding to the plague for the reason we described, and to avoid leaving it for the reason we mentioned. Likewise what we narrated at the start: No sick one is brought to a healthy one - lest the healthy be afflicted and the bringer say: had I not brought him, nothing would have struck him - though without bringing, it might equally have struck him. So he was commanded to avoid bringing the healthy to the sick, for this cause people may fall into in hearts and words."
"6626","7049 - And there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) negating contagion what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Hadrami, that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib said: I asked Sa'd about omens, and he rebuked me, saying: Who narrated to you? I disliked telling him. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no contagion and no omen."
"6627","7050 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the same, adding: nor hāmah (owl omen)."
"6628","7051 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us. 7052 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, they said: al-Walid ibn Uqbah al-Shaybani narrated to us, he said: Hamzah al-Zayyat narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Tha'labah ibn Yazid al-Himmani, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No sick one infects a healthy one."
"6629","7053 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no omen, no hāmah, and no contagion. A man said: A mangy sheep is cast among the flock and infects them all? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and Ibn Abbas - said: Then who infected the first?"
"6630","7054 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Simak, then he mentioned with his chain the same, without doubting any of it, all from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6631","7055 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ibn Shubrumah, from Abu Zur'ah ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no contagion. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, a camel free of mange stands beside a camel and mange covers all the camels? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then who infected the first? Allah created every creature, wrote its term, its provision, and its trace."
"6632","7056 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Umarah ibn al-Qa'qa', from Abu Zur'ah, from a man, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6633","7057 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ibrahim al-Kirmani narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Masruq narrated to us, from Umarah, from Abu Zur'ah, from a man among the Prophet's companions, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6634","7058 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Umarah ibn al-Qa'qa', from Abu Zur'ah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6635","7059 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik and Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Hamzah and Salim, sons of Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: There is no contagion."
"6636","7060 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj. 7061 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, that Abu al-Zubayr narrated to him, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6637","7062 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6638","7063 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6639","7064 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan informed me, he said: al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, Zayd ibn Aslam, and Ubayd Allah ibn Miqsam narrated to me, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Adding: nor hāmah, nor ghoul, nor safar. Abu Salih said: I traveled to Kufah then returned, and Abu Hurayrah was omitting: There is no contagion, not mentioning it. I said: Nor contagion? He said: I refuse?"
"6640","7065 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Salamah and others informed me that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion. A Bedouin said: O Messenger of Allah, what about camels in the sand like gazelles, then a mangy camel comes and infects them? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then who infected the first?"
"6641","7066 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6642","7067 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ma'ruf ibn Suwayd al-Judhami informed me, from Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion."
"6643","7068 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, nephew of Namir, informed me, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6644","7069 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hisham and Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6645","7070 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, he said: I heard Abu al-Rabi' narrate from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Four things in my ummah are from pre-Islamic ignorance, people will never abandon them: reviling lineages, wailing, (saying) we were rained by such and such star, and contagion - a camel among camels becomes mangy, and he says: who infected the first."
"6646","7071 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Alqamah, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6647","7072 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Ashajj narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no contagion, and he said: Then who infected the first?"
"6648","7073 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Mufaddal ibn Fudalah, from Habib ibn al-Shahid, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a leper's hand and placed it in the bowl, saying: In Allah's name, trusting in Allah, relying upon Allah."
"6649","7074 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6650","7075 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Muslim al-Khawlani, from Abu Dharr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stay with the afflicted companion, in humility to your Lord, and in faith. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thus negated contagion in these reports we mentioned, saying: Then who infected the first - meaning: had the second been afflicted merely because the first infected him, the first would have had nothing, having no infector. Since what struck the first was by Allah's decree, so was what struck the second. If one says: make this contradict his saying: No sick one is brought to a healthy one, as Abu Hurayrah did. I say: no; rather his saying: There is no contagion, is a denial that contagion ever occurs, while his saying: No sick one is brought to a healthy one, concerns fear that he be brought and afflicted by Allah's decree like the first, so people say: the first infected him. So bringing the healthy to the sick was disliked, fearing this saying. We also narrated his placing the leper's hand in the bowl, proving negation of contagion, for had contagion been possible, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have done what risks it, bringing ruin upon himself, which Allah forbade, saying: And do not kill yourselves. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed a leaning target and hastened; since he hastened from a leaning target fearing death, how could he do what risks contagion? I mentioned earlier also the meaning of his forbidding descending upon plague and leaving it: forbidding descending lest it be in Allah's prior knowledge that descending afflicts them, so they say: we were afflicted because we descended; and forbidding leaving lest one leave safe and say: I was safe because I left. Since both prohibitions share one meaning - omens, not contagion - likewise his saying: No sick one is brought to a healthy one, is about omens, not contagion. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade in all these the causes of ominous thinking. And in Usamah's hadith we narrated: and when it strikes a land while he is in it, let not fleeing expel him, is proof there is no harm in leaving it for other than fleeing."
"6651","7076 - And what proves that is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, that some men of Ukl came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah and found it unhealthy. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you gone out to our camels and drunk from their milk and urine. They did and recovered, then he mentioned the hadith."
"6652","7077 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Some sick men of an Arab tribe came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), embraced Islam and pledged to him, while illness - namely pleurisy - had struck. They said: O Messenger of Allah, this ailment has struck; would you permit us to go out to the camels and stay with them. He said: Yes, go out and stay with them. In this hadith, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to go out to the camels though epidemic had struck Madinah; that was - in our view, and Allah knows best - for treatment, not fleeing. Thus leaving plague-stricken land is disliked when fleeing, permitted otherwise. In this sense - and Allah knows best - Umar returned with the people from Sargh, not as fleeing what had befallen them."
"6653","7078 - And the proof is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Allah, people attribute three traits to me and I disavow them to You: they claim I fled from the plague, and I disavow that to You; that I permitted them distilled drink, which is wine, and I disavow that to You; and that I permitted them the tax, which is filth, and I disavow that to You. This Umar informs he disavows fleeing the plague, proving his return was for another matter. Likewise his letter to Abu Ubaydah to leave with the Muslim troops was for al-Jabiyah's wholesomeness and al-Urdunn's lowness. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari clarified in Shu'bah's hadith what is disliked in plague: one leaves and is safe, saying: I was safe because I left; or descends upon it and is afflicted, saying: I was afflicted because I descended. And Abu Musa permitted people to withdraw if they wished, proving the explanation we described. This is the meaning of these reports in our view, and Allah knows best. As for omens, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abolished them, with continuously transmitted reports."
"6654","7079 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir and Rawh narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Isa, a man of Banu Asad, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Omens are from polytheism, and there is none among us but - Allah removes it by reliance."
"6655","7080 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ibn Shubrumah, from Abu Zur'ah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no omen."
"6656","7081 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Umarah ibn al-Qa'qa', from Abu Zur'ah, from a man, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6657","7082 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Hamzah and Salim, sons of Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6658","7083 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, he said: Alqamah ibn Abi Alqamah narrated to me, from his mother, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hated omens and disliked them."
"6659","7084 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hisham and Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no omen."
"6660","7085 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Salamah and others informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6661","7086 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6662","7087 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ma'ruf ibn Suwayd informed me, from Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6663","7088 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6664","7089 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6665","7090 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Ashajj narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid narrated to me, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6666","7091 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Awf, from Hayyan, from Qatan ibn Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq, from his father, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Divination by birds, omens, and divination by lines are from sorcery. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade omens and reported they are from polytheism, he forbade people the causes from which omens arise, mentioned in this chapter. If one says: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Evil omen is in three things. It was said: that is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as you mentioned."
"6667","7092 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Hamzah and Salim, sons of Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Evil omen is only in three: in woman, horse, and house."
"6668","7093 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6669","7094 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the same, except he did not mention Hamzah."
"6670","7095 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim informed me that Abd Allah ibn Umar said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say. Then he mentioned the same."
"6671","7096 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Utbah ibn Muslim informed me, from Hamzah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it (the previous report). And it has also been reported with a meaning contrary to this, from the hadith of Ibn Umar and others."
"6672","7097 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Hadrami, that Said ibn al-Musayyib said: I asked Sad ibn Malik about tiyarah (taking bad omens), so he rebuked me and said: Who told you this? So I disliked telling him, then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no tiyarah, but if tiyarah exists in anything, then it is in the woman, the house, and the horse."
"6673","7098 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Utbah ibn Muslim narrated to me, from Hamzah ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: If bad omen is in anything, then it is in three: in the horse, the dwelling, and the woman."
"6674","7099 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, he heard Jabir narrating, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"6675","7100 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, that he heard Sahl ibn Sad narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as it. Abu Hazim said: But Sahl ibn Sad did not seem to affirm it, while the people affirm it."
"6676","7101 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us that al-Hadrami ibn Lahiq narrated to him, that Said ibn al-Musayyib narrated to him, he said: I asked Sad about tiyarah, so he rebuked me and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no tiyarah, but if tiyarah is in anything, then it is in the woman, the house, and the horse."
"6677","7102 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Utbah ibn Humayd, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, that he heard Anas ibn Malik narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as it."
"6678","7103 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: If bad omen is in anything, then it is in three: in the woman, the horse, and the house."
"6679","7104 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Abu Layla, from Atiyyah, from Abu Said that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion and no tiyarah, but if it is in anything, then it is in the woman, the horse, and the house. In this hadith is what indicates other than what is in the section before this section. That is because Sad rebuked Said when he mentioned tiyarah to him, and informed him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: There is no tiyarah, then he said: If tiyarah is in anything, then it is in the woman, the horse, and the house. So he did not inform that it is in them, but rather said: If it were in anything, it would be in them, meaning: if it were to exist in anything, it would be in these, so if it is not in these three, then it is not in anything. And it has been reported from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about that was with a different wording."
"6680","7105 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan, he said: Two men from Banu Amir entered upon Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and informed her that Abu Hurayrah narrates from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Tiyarah is in the woman, the house, and the horse. So she became angry, and one part of her flew to the sky and a part to the earth out of anger, and she said: By the One Who sent down the Quran upon Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never said it at all; he only said: The people of Jahiliyyah used to take bad omens from that. So Aishah informed that that statement was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as a narration about the people of Jahiliyyah, because it was so in his view."
"6681","Chapter 23 - The chapter of choosing between the prophets, peace be upon them. 7106 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful, he said: I heard Anas saying: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O best of creation, so he said: That is my father Ibrahim, peace be upon him."
"6682","7107 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"6683","7108 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, they said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain."
"6684","7109 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as it. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that there is no harm in preferring between the prophets, so it is said: So-and-so is better than so-and-so, according to what is found in each one of them. And others differed with them in that, and disliked preferring between the prophets."
"6685","7110 - And they argued in that with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not prefer between the prophets of Allah."
"6686","7111 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Amr ibn Yahya ibn Umarah, from his father, from Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as it."
"6687","7112 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain."
"6688","7113 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Majishun narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Fadl, he said: al-Araj informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the same as it, in a long hadith, except that he said: Do not prefer (one over another). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade preferring between the prophets. And it was reported from him that he said: Do not prefer me over Musa. From Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not prefer me over Musa, for the people will fall unconscious on the Day of Resurrection, and I will be the first to recover, and then Musa, peace be upon him, will be holding the side of the Throne, so I will not know whether he fell unconscious among those who fell unconscious and recovered before me, or he was among those whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, exempted. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that they prefer him over Musa, and said to them: I am the first to recover from the swoon, then I find Musa standing, so I do not know whether he was among those who fell unconscious before me and recovered before me, or he was among those whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, exempted. That was, in our view, because it was possible in his view that he was among those whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, exempted, so the swoon did not strike him, and so he was preferred by that, or he fell unconscious and recovered before him, so he was in his rank, because both had fallen unconscious together. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked for that reason preferring him over him, because of the possibility that the swoon passed over him. And it has also been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: It is not fitting for anyone to say: I am better than Yunus ibn Matta."
"6689","7114 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: It is not fitting for anyone to say: I am better than Yunus ibn Matta."
"6690","7115 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, he said: I heard Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrating from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: It is not fitting for a servant to say: I am better than Yunus ibn Matta."
"6691","7116 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Salamah narrating from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), as if from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and he mentioned the same as it, and added: He (Yunus) had glorified Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in the darknesses. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade preferring between him and any one of the prophets specifically, and informed of a virtue for each one he mentioned of them that was not for others. If one says: does this contradict the hadith of al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful? I say: this is not, in my view, contradictory to it, because the hadith of al-Mukhtar is only that Ibrahim is the best of creation, and it did not single out anyone over anyone. And in the other reports, there is preferring one prophet over another prophet, so preferring one of them specifically over another of them disparages the one deemed less excellent, while preferring a man over people does not disparage any of them. This may be the meaning, so that these reports do not contradict. And it may be that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, informed His Messenger that Ibrahim, peace be upon him, is the best of creation, and did not inform him about preferring some of the other prophets over others. So he stopped where Allah did not inform him, and commanded stopping there, and spoke freely about what Allah had informed him of."
"6692","Chapter 24 - The chapter of castrating livestock. 7117 - Abu Khalid Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade castrating camels, cattle, sheep, and horses. And Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: From them creation proceeds, and females are not sound except with males."
"6693","7118 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Nafi, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to this and said: It is not lawful to castrate any stallions, and they argued in that with this hadith, and with the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: They will surely change the creation of Allah. They said: and it is castration. And others differed with them in that and said: Whatever livestock whose biting is feared, or whose fattening is intended, there is no harm in castrating it. And they said: This hadith with which our opponent argued against us is only from Ibn Umar as a stopped report, and is not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6694","7119 - So they mentioned what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) the same as it, and he did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the people of this hadith hold that it is only from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for what they mentioned of the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: They will surely change the creation of Allah, it has been said: its interpretation is what they held. And it has been said: it is the religion of Allah. And we have seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrifice two gelded rams, and they are the ones whose testicles were crushed, and what is done with that has its progeny cut off. So if castrating them were disliked, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have sacrificed them, so that people would refrain from that and not do it, because when they know that what is castrated is avoided, they will desist from that and not do it. Do you not see that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, in what we reported from him in the chapter of riding mules, was brought a castrated slave to buy, and said: I would not help in castration. So he made buying him a help in his castration, because had it not been for one who buys him because he is castrated, the one who castrated him would not have castrated him. Likewise the castration of sheep: if it were disliked, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have sacrificed what had been castrated of them. And the castration of livestock does not resemble the castration of the children of Adam, because the castration of livestock is only intended for what we mentioned, of their fattening and stopping their biting, so that is permitted. But the children of Adam, castrating them is only intended for sins, so that is not permitted. And if what we reported at the beginning of this chapter were authentic, it could mean the castration by which the production of creation is cut off is disliked. As for castration by which the production of creation is not cut off, it is otherwise. And it has been reported about the permissibility of castrating livestock from a group of the predecessors."
"6695","7120 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah that he castrated a mule of his."
"6696","7121 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, the same as it."
"6697","7122 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus that his father castrated a camel of his."
"6698","7123 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from Ata, he said: There is no harm in castrating the stallion if its biting is feared."
"6699","Chapter 25 - The chapter of writing knowledge: is it proper or not? 7124 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd, from his father, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri that he sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to write knowledge, but he did not permit him. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to disliking the writing of knowledge, and they forbade that, and they argued in it with what we mentioned. And others differed with them in that, and saw no harm in writing knowledge, and they countered what their opponent argued against them with, of the report we mentioned, with what has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6700","7125 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Mukhariq, from Tariq, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed us and said: We have no book that we read to you except the Book of Allah, and this sheet, meaning the sheet in his inkpot. And he said: in the sheath of his sword, we took it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); in it are the ordinances of charity."
"6701","7126 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Ali, he said: We have nothing from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in writing except the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and something in this sheet: Madinah is sacred, between Ayr and Thawr. And in the hadith there is more than this."
"6702","7127 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from al-Mughirah ibn Hakim and Mujahid, that they heard Abu Hurayrah saying: There was no one more retentive of the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than me, except what Abdullah ibn Amr was, for I retained with my heart, and he retained with his heart and wrote with his hand; he sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for that, and he permitted him."
"6703","7128 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salman informed me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that Shuayb narrated to him and Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I write what I hear from you? He said: Yes. I said: At anger and pleasure? He said: It is not fitting for me to say except the truth."
"6704","7129 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: And he informed me, meaning Abd al-Rahman ibn Salman, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from al-Mughirah ibn Hakim, that he heard from Abu Hurayrah, and he mentioned similar to that."
"6705","7130 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Uthman ibn Ata, from his father, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I hear things from you that I fear I will forget; will you permit me to write them? He said: Yes. So in these reports is the permission to write knowledge, contrary to the hadith of Abu Said which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter. And this is worthier by consideration, because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said about debt: And do not be weary to write it, small or large, to its term; that is more just with Allah and more upright for testimony and nearer that you not doubt. So when Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded writing debt for fear of doubt, knowledge whose memorization is harder than memorizing debt is worthier that its writing be permitted, for fear of doubt and uncertainty in it. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah, Most High, have mercy on them. And there has also been reported in that from those after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what agrees with this."
"6706","7131 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Umar al-Adani narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Aban narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that some people of Taif came to him with sheets of his sheets, to read them to them. When he took them he could not proceed, so he said: When my sight went I became forgetful, so read them to me, and let there be no unease in yourselves from that, for your reading to me is like my reading to you."
"6707","7132 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Tawus, he said: Said ibn Jubayr used to write in the presence of Ibn Abbas, and it was said to him: They are writing, so he said: Let them write, and he was the best of them in character."
"6708","7133 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Yaqub al-Qummi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil narrated to us, he said: We used to come to Jabir ibn Abdullah, and ask him about the sunnahs of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we would write them."
"6709","7134 - Husayn narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, he said: Mahmud ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Itban ibn Malik, Anas said: So I met Itban, and he narrated it to me, and it pleased me, so I said to my son: Write it, so he wrote it."
"6710","7135 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, (Ha). 7136 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, from his brother: he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: There is no one among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with more hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than me, except Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him), for he used to write and I did not write."
"6711","7137 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Ishaq al-Dimashqi narrated to us, from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Abu Mijlaz, from Bashir ibn Nahik, he said: I used to take books from Abu Hurayrah and write them, then when I finished, I read them to him, and I would say: What I read to you, did you hear it from you (i.e. is it what you narrated)? So he would say: Yes."
"6712","Chapter 26 - The chapter of cauterization: is it disliked or not? 7138 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, that some people brought a companion of theirs to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and asked him: Shall we cauterize him? So he was silent, then they asked him and he was silent, then they asked him and he said: Pound (medicine) for him with hot stones, or burn him, and he disliked that."
"6713","7139 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: Three men came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: A companion of ours is ill and cauterization was described for him, shall we cauterize him? So he was silent, then they repeated and he was silent, then he said to them the third time: Cauterize him if you wish, and if you wish, treat him with heated stones. Abu Jafar said: The meaning of this, in our view, is a threat whose outward form is a command but whose inward meaning is prohibition, as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: And incite whomever you can of them, and as His saying: Do what you will."
"6714","7140 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abu Said Muhammad ibn Asad al-Taghlibi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: If there is healing in anything you treat with, then it is in a cupping incision, or a drink of honey, or a touch of fire, and I do not like to be cauterized."
"6715","7141 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seventy thousand of my ummah will enter Paradise without reckoning. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, who are they? He said: They are those who do not take bad omens, nor cauterize, nor seek ruqyah, and upon their Lord they rely."
"6716","7142 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: We were forbidden from cauterization."
"6717","7143 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu Hubayrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from Uqbah ibn Amir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade cauterization. So a group went to the view that cauterization is disliked, and that it is not permitted for anyone to do it in any case, and they argued in that with these reports. And others differed with them in that and said: There is no harm in cauterization for what cauterization treats. And among the proof for them in that was what:"
"6718","7144 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: Ubayy ibn Kab complained (of illness), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a physician to him, who cut a vein of his, then cauterized it."
"6719","7145 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a physician to Ubayy ibn Kab, who cut a vein of his, then cauterized it."
"6720","7146 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: Ubayy ibn Kab complained, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a physician to him, who slit his basilic vein and cauterized it."
"6721","7147 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: Sad ibn Muadh was struck in his basilic vein, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped its bleeding with his hand with an arrowhead, then it swelled, so he cauterized it a second time."
"6722","7148 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Ibn al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that Ubayy ibn Kab, or Sad, was struck with a wound in his hand, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a physician who cauterized him over it."
"6723","7149 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: On the day of al-Ahzab, Sad ibn Muadh was struck and they cut his basilic vein, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cauterized it with fire, then his hand swelled, so he cauterized it another time."
"6724","7150 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cauterized Asad ibn Zurarah for a thorn."
"6725","7151 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Dhuray narrated to us, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain, except that he said: for pleurisy pain."
"6726","7152 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Imran narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: Abu Talhah cauterized me, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among us, and I was not forbidden from it."
"6727","7153 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from one of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cauterized Sad, or Asad ibn Zurarah, for throat inflammation in his throat. In these reports is the permission of cauterization for the ailment mentioned in them, while in the first reports is the prohibition of cauterization. So it is possible that the meaning for which there was permission in these reports is other than the meaning for which there was prohibition in the first reports. That is because some people used to cauterize before affliction befell them, thinking that would prevent affliction from befalling them, as the non-Arabs do. So this is disliked, because it is not by way of treatment, and it is shirk, because they do it to repel the decree of Allah from them. As for what is after the affliction befalls, it is only intended for remedy, and treatment is permitted and commanded. And Jabir ibn Abdullah explained that in a hadith he reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6728","7154 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Asim ibn Umar, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If there is good in any of these medicines of yours, then it is in a cupping incision, or a drink of honey, or a touch of fire that matches the ailment, and I do not like to be cauterized. Since in this hadith the touch of fire that matches the ailment is permitted, while cauterization (in general) is disliked, and the touch of fire is a cauterization, it is established that the cauterization that matches the ailment is permitted, and the cauterization that does not match the ailment is disliked. And it is possible that cauterization was forbidden according to the first reports, then permitted after that according to these later reports."
"6729","7155 - And that is because Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Sulaym, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking permission for cauterization, so he said: Do not cauterize. So he said: O Messenger of Allah, hardship has reached me, and I find no escape from being cauterized. He said: As you wish, but there is no wound except that it will come to Allah on the Day of Resurrection bleeding, complaining of the pain that caused it, and the wound of cauterization will come on the Day of Resurrection mentioning that its cause was from dislike of meeting Allah, then he ordered him to be cauterized. In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade cauterization and then permitted it after that. So it is possible that what is in the first reports was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the state of prohibition mentioned in this hadith. And what was of permission in the other reports was after the permission from him mentioned in this hadith, so the permission abrogates the prohibition. And it has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he cauterized a thief after cutting him."
"6730","7156 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrated to us, from Makhul, from Ibn Muhayriz, he said: I said to Fadalah ibn Ubayd: Is it of the Sunnah that the thief be cut and it (the hand) be hung on his neck? So he said: Yes, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a thief, so he ordered him, his hand was cut, then he cauterized it, then he hung it on his neck."
"6731","7157 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a man who stole a shawl, so he said: Did you steal? I do not think you stole. Take him and cut him, then cauterize him, then he said: Repent to Allah. In this also is proof of the permission of cauterization intended for treatment, because it is medicine. And the Bedouins asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Shall we not treat? So his answer to them in that was what:"
"6732","7158 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Ilaqah narrated to us, he said: I heard Usamah ibn Sharik saying: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while the Bedouins were asking him, saying: Is there sin upon us if we treat? So he said: Treat, O servants of Allah, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, did not place a disease except that He placed for it a cure, except old age."
"6733","7159 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn Amr narrated to me, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, treat, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, did not create a disease except that He created for it a healing, except al-sam, and al-sam is death."
"6734","7160 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Abd Rabbih ibn Said, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: For every disease there is a medicine, so when the medicine of the disease is hit, he recovers, by the permission of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them to treat, and cauterization is among what they used to treat with. And the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cauterized after him. From those reported about that is what:"
"6735","7161 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abjar narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Jarir, he said: Umar swore upon me that I should surely be cauterized."
"6736","7162 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: I saw Abdullah ibn Umar cauterized for facial palsy at the root of his ears."
"6737","7163 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was cauterized for facial palsy."
"6738","7164 - Shuayb ibn Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Abu Hanifah narrated to us, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was cauterized for facial palsy, and was given ruqyah for the scorpion."
"6739","7165 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), the same as it."
"6740","7166 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, he said: I entered upon Khabbab, and he had been cauterized."
"6741","7167 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Ismail, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Khabbab, that he came to him visiting him, and he had been cauterized seven times in his belly."
"6742","7168 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard Humayd - Ibn Marzuq said: I think it is from Mutarrif, he said: Imran ibn Husayn said to me: Did you know that greeting used to be given to me, then when I was cauterized the greeting ceased from me. These are the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who were cauterized and cauterized others, and among them is Ibn Umar, and we have reported from him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not like to be cauterized. So his doing that indicates the abrogation of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had disliked of that is established. And among them is Imran ibn Husayn, who reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his praise of those who do not cauterize. So that also indicates his knowledge of the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for that. If one says: how can that be when it has been reported from Imran ibn Husayn? And he mentioned what:"
"6743","7169 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Jabir narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Hudayr narrated to us, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: Imran ibn Husayn used to forbid cauterization, then he was afflicted, and he used to say: I was cauterized with fire and it neither absolved me of sin nor healed me of sickness. It was said to him: It may be that the cauterization Imran used to forbid is the cauterization intended not for treatment of an affliction that has befallen, but for what is done before the affliction befalls, which they thought repels affliction. Then when he was afflicted with what he was afflicted with, he was cauterized on the basis that it was treatment for his affliction. When he did not recover by that, he knew his cauterization did not match his affliction, and was not treatment for it, so he feared it brought sin, and said: It neither healed me of sickness nor absolved me of sin, meaning: I do not know that I am free of sin, though he did not assert that he had become sinful by it, because he only intended by it medicine and nothing else, and medicine is permitted for all people, and they are commanded with it. And there have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reports forbidding amulets. From what was reported in that is what:"
"6744","7170 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan, she said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of mine, and I had hung something on him for throat inflammation, so he said: Why do you torment your children with this hanging? Upon you is this Indian aloewood, for in it are seven healings, among them pleurisy: it is snuffed for throat inflammation, and given in the side of the mouth for pleurisy. It may be that that hanging was disliked in itself, because there was written in it what is not lawful to write, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked it for that, not for other than it. And there has also been reported in that what:"
"6745","7171 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Ubaydullah ibn Zahr, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from a man of Suda, he said: We came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), twelve men, and we pledged to him, but he left one man of us and did not pledge him. So we said: Pledge him, O Prophet of Allah, so he said: I will not pledge him until he removes what is on him; whoever of us has on him like what is on him is a mushrik as long as it is on him. So we looked, and there was on his upper arm a strap of tree bark, or something of magic."
"6746","7172 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from Haywah, he said: Khalid ibn Ubayd informed me, he said: I heard Mishrah ibn Haan saying: I heard Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever hangs an amulet, may Allah not complete for him, and whoever hangs a shell, may Allah not protect him."
"6747","7173 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abbad ibn Tamim, that Abu Bishr al-Ansari informed him, that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of his journeys - Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr said: I thought he said: - while the people were in their night stop, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a caller: No necklace nor bowstring shall remain on the neck of a camel except that it is cut. Malik said: I think that was because of the evil eye. That was, in our view, and Allah knows best, what was hung before the affliction befell, to repel it, and that is what none but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, can do, so he forbade that, because it is shirk. As for what is after the affliction befalls, there is no harm, because it is treatment. And this same statement has been reported from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her)."
"6748","7174 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: It is not an amulet: what is hung after the affliction occurs."
"6749","7175 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak, from Talhah ibn Abi Said, or Sad, from Bukayr, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain. So it is also possible that cauterization was forbidden when done before the affliction befalls, and permitted when done after the affliction befalls, because what is done after the affliction befalls is only treatment. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about treatment what we have mentioned in this chapter. And there has also been reported from him what:"
"6750","7176 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a healing, so upon you is cow's milk, for it grazes from every tree."
"6751","7177 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Abu Hanifah narrated to us, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain. And a group disliked ruqyah, and they argued in that with the hadith of Imran ibn Husayn which we mentioned in the first section. And others differed with them in that, and saw no harm in it. And they argued in that with what:"
"6752","7178 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he permitted ruqyah for the snake and the scorpion. In this hadith is the permission for ruqyah for the snake and the scorpion, and permission only comes after prohibition. So that indicates that what was permitted of that abrogates the prohibition of it in the hadith of Imran. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about commanding ruqyah for the scorpion sting what:"
"6753","7179 - Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Mulazim ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Badr narrated to us, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a scorpion stung me, so he began wiping it and performing ruqyah for it."
"6754","7180 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib narrated to us, he said: Mulazim narrated to us, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain."
"6755","7181 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: A scorpion stung a man of us in the presence of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So a man said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I perform ruqyah for him? So he said: Whoever of you can benefit his brother, let him do so."
"6756","7182 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, similar to it. In the hadith of Jabir is what indicates that every ruqyah in which there is benefit is permitted, because of the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever of you can benefit his brother, let him do so. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about permitting ruqyah for ant bites."
"6757","7183 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Hathmah, from al-Shifa, a woman, who was a cousin of Umar, she said: I was with Hafsah, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us, and said: Will you not teach her the ruqyah for the ant bite, as you taught her writing?"
"6758","7184 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Abu Bakr ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah, from Hafsah, that a woman of Quraysh called al-Shifa used to perform ruqyah for ant bites, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Teach it to Hafsah. In this hadith is the permission of ruqyah for ant bites. So it may be that was after the prohibition, so it abrogates the prohibition, or the prohibition was after it, so it abrogates it. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about permitting ruqyah for madness what:"
"6759","7185 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Umayr, the freedman of Abi al-Lahm, he said: I presented to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a ruqyah with which I used to treat madness, so he commanded me with part of it and forbade me from part of it, and I used to perform ruqyah with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me with. This also bears what we mentioned about what was reported on ruqyah for ant bites. And there has been reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about ruqyah for the evil eye what:"
"6760","7186 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mabad ibn Khalid, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to seek ruqyah for the evil eye."
"6761","7187 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mabad, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), the same as it. Or he said: Abdullah ibn Shaddad said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) to seek ruqyah for the evil eye."
"6762","7188 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq ibn Hammam narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Asma bint Umays: Why do I see the bodies of my brother's sons thin and stricken? Are they afflicted by need? She said: No, but the evil eye hastens to them, so I perform ruqyah for them. He said: With what? So she presented to him words with no harm in them, so he said: Perform ruqyah for them."
"6763","7189 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan and Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, they said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Abdullah ibn Babah, from Asma bint Umays, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, the evil eye hastens to the sons of Jafar, shall I seek ruqyah for them? He said: Yes, for if anything were to precede the decree, I would say the evil eye precedes it. This bears what we mentioned about the ruqyah for ant bites and madness. And there has also been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the permission for ruqyah for every venomous sting."
"6764","7190 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted ruqyah for every venomous sting."
"6765","7191 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain. This contains proof that it was after the prohibition, because permission only comes from something prohibited. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about permitting all ruqyahs as long as they are not shirk what:"
"6766","7192 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Awf ibn Malik al-Ashjai, he said: We used to perform ruqyah in Jahiliyyah. So we said: O Messenger of Allah, we used to perform ruqyah in Jahiliyyah, what do you think of that? He said: Present your ruqyahs to me; there is no harm in ruqyah as long as it is not shirk. This also bears what was borne by what we reported before it, so we needed to know: is this permission for ruqyah later than what was reported of prohibition of it, or is what was reported of prohibition of it later than it, so it abrogates it? So we examined that."
"6767","7193 - Then Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, that Amr ibn Hazm was called for a woman in Madinah whom a snake bit, to perform ruqyah for her, but he refused. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed of that, and he called him. So Amr said: O Messenger of Allah, you forbid ruqyah, so he said: Recite it to me, so he recited it to him, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no harm in it; they are only covenants, so perform ruqyah with it."
"6768","7194 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade ruqyah, my maternal uncle came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, you forbade ruqyah, yet I perform ruqyah for the scorpion. He said: Whoever of you can benefit his brother, let him do so."
"6769","7195 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: A household of the Ansar used to perform ruqyah for the snake, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade ruqyah. Then a man came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I used to perform ruqyah for the scorpion, yet you forbade ruqyah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever of you can benefit his brother, let him do so. He said: And a man came to him who used to perform ruqyah for the snake, and said: Present it to me, so he presented it to him, and he said: There is no harm in it; they are only covenants. So it is established by what we mentioned that what was reported on permitting ruqyah abrogates what was reported on forbidding it. Then we wanted to examine those ruqyahs, how they are. Then Awf ibn Malik reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that also that there is no harm in them as long as they are not shirk. And there has also been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what:"
"6770","7196 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: al-Rabab narrated to me, she said: I heard Sahl ibn Hunayf saying: We passed by a stream and entered to bathe, then I came out of it feverish, so that was reported to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Order Abu Thabit to seek refuge. So I said: O my master, is ruqyah proper? So he said: There is no ruqyah except for three: for the evil eye glance, venomous sting, and bite. It may be that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted of ruqyah is seeking refuge. As for the saying of Sahl: There is no ruqyah except for three, it may be that he knew that from the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after his earlier prohibition, and did not know other than that of what we reported from others, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted."
"6771","7197 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Nadrah narrated to us, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that Jibril came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Did you complain, O Muhammad? He said: Yes. He said: In the name of Allah I perform ruqyah for you, from everything that harms you, from the evil of every soul and breath and eye; Allah heals you, in the name of Allah I perform ruqyah for you."
"6772","7198 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Azhar ibn Said, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Saib, the nephew of Maymunah, he said: Maymunah said to him: Shall I not perform ruqyah for you with the ruqyah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. She said: In the name of Allah I perform ruqyah for you, and Allah heals you, from every ailment in you: remove the harm, Lord of people, and heal, You are the Healer, there is no healer but You. This and what resembles it of ruqyahs, there is no harm in it. And the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Awf also indicates that: There is no harm in ruqyah as long as it is not shirk. So that indicates that every ruqyah in which there is no shirk is not disliked, and Allah knows best."
"6773","Chapter 27 - The chapter of talking after the last Isha. 7199 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Rifah al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sayyar ibn Salamah, he said: I entered with my father upon Abu Barzah, and I heard him saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to dislike sleep before the last Isha, and talking after it."
"6774","7200 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Sayyar, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to disliking talk after the last Isha, and they argued in that with this hadith. And others differed with them in that and said: As for speech that is not a means of nearness to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, even if it is not disobedience, it is disliked then, because it is recommended for a man to sleep upon nearness and good and merit with which he seals his deed. So the best thing for him is to sleep upon prayer, so that it is his last deed."
"6775","7201 - And they argued for the permissibility of talk after Isha with what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Wail, he said: He said: Abdullah narrated to us, (Ha)."
"6776","7202 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Wail, he said: Abdullah narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) encouraged for us staying awake talking after the late night prayer. And Muslim said: After the Isha prayer. In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) encouraged for them talking after the last Isha, while in the first hadith he disliked that. Their reconciliation, in our view, and Allah knows best, is that he disliked for them such talking as is not nearness, and encouraged for them what is nearness, upon the meaning we mentioned from the people of the second view mentioned in this chapter."
"6777","7203 - And Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, Abu al-Walid said, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes stay awake talking in the house of Abu Bakr one night about a matter of the Muslims. This hadith clarifies the talking of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which he used to engage in, and that it was about Muslim affairs, and that is among the greatest acts of obedience. So that indicates that the talking forbidden is other than this."
"6778","7204 - And there has also been reported in that from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah, he said: Umar encouraged for us talking after the last Isha. In this hadith Umar encouraged them to talk after the last Isha, but did not clarify to us in this hadith which talking that was, so we examined that."
"6779","7205 - Then Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, he said: I heard Abu Nadrah narrating from Abu Said, the freedman of the Ansar, he said: Umar would not leave anyone staying awake talking after Isha, saying: Go back, perhaps Allah will grant you prayer or tahajjud. Then he came to us while I was sitting with Ibn Masud, Ubayy ibn Kab, and Abu Dharr, and said: What keeps you seated? We said: We intended to remember Allah, so he sat with them. This is Umar: he used to forbid them talking after Isha so they would return to their houses, to pray, or to sleep, then rise for prayer, being thereby those who pray at night. When he asked them what kept them seated, and they informed him it was remembrance of Allah, he did not disapprove of that and sat with them, because what he would keep them up for is what they were seated for. So it is established thereby that the talking in the hadith of Abu Wail from Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Umar encouraged, is that in which there is nearness to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the prohibition in the hadith of Abu Barzah is of what has no nearness in it, so that the meanings of these reports align, agree, and do not contradict. And we have reported from Abdullah ibn Abbas and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah that they talked until the Pleiades rose. That is, in our view, upon talking that is nearness to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and we have mentioned that hadith with its chain earlier in this book of ours. And it has also been reported from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) through a chain not firmly established, that she said: There is no talking except for one praying or a traveler. That, in our view, if established from her, does not contradict what we reported, because the traveler needs what repels sleep from him so he can travel, so talking was permitted thereby even if it is not nearness, as long as it is not disobedience, due to his need for that. This is the meaning of her saying: There is no talking except for the traveler. As for her saying: or one praying, its meaning, in our view, is upon one who prays after talking, so his sleep when he sleeps after that is upon prayer, not upon talking. So this meaning returns to the meaning to which we directed the meanings of the first reports, and Allah knows best."
"6780","Chapter 28 - The chapter of a slave looking at the hair of free women. 7206 - al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Nabhan, the freedman of Umm Salamah, from Umm Salamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you has a mukatab (contracted slave), and he has what to pay, then let her veil from him. Sufyan said: I heard it from al-Zuhri, and Mamar confirmed it to me. Abu Jafar said: So a group of the people of Madinah went to the view that there is no harm in a slave looking at the hair of his mistress and her face, and at what her unmarriageable kin look at of her. And they argued in that with this hadith, and said about the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Umm Salamah, let her veil from him: it proves she had been before that unveiled from him. And they said: That has been reported from Ibn Abbas, and the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) acted upon it after him. They mentioned in that what:"
"6781","7207 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abu Malik, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is no harm in a slave looking at the hair of his mistress."
"6782","7208 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Maymun ibn Yahya informed me, from the family of al-Ashajj, from Makhramah ibn Bukayr, from his father, from Amr ibn Shuayb, Yazid ibn Abdullah, and Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, that they said: If a woman sat with her husband's slave without a headcover, there would be no harm in that. 7209 - Bukayr said: And Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim informed me that Asma bint Abd al-Rahman used to sit with a slave of Qasim, who was her husband, without a headcover. 7210 - Bukayr said, from Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman, she said: Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) used to be seen by slaves belonging to others. 7211 - Bakr said: Umm Alqamah, the freedwoman of Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), said: She would admit Muslim slaves to her, and the slaves of people would see Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) after one of them reached puberty, while she was combing her hair. 7212 - Bukayr said, from Abdullah ibn Rafi: Umm Salamah did not veil from the slaves of people. And others differed with them in that and said: A slave looks at a free woman only at what a free man with no unmarriageable kinship between him and her looks at of her. And among the proof for them in that was that the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in the hadith of Umm Salamah does not indicate what the people of that view said, because it may mean the veiling of the Mothers of the Believers, for they had been veiled from all people except those of unmarriageable kin. So it was not permitted for anyone to see them at all except one between whom and them was unmarriageable kinship. Other women are not so, because there is no harm in a man looking at a woman with whom he has no kinship and who is not forbidden to him, at her face and hands. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: And let them not display their adornment except what appears of it. It has been said in that what:"
"6783","7213 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah: And let them not display their adornment except what appears of it. He said: Adornment is the earring, necklace, bracelet, anklet, and armlet. What appears of it: clothes and outer cloak."
"6784","7214 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Muslim, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: And let them not display their adornment except what appears of it: kohl and the ring."
"6785","7215 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: And let them not display their adornment except what appears of it, he said: It is what is above the shirt, so people were permitted to look at what is not forbidden to them of women, at their faces and hands. And that was forbidden to them regarding the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the verse of hijab came down, so they were distinguished thereby over other women."
"6786","7216 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, he said: Umar said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, the righteous and the wicked enter upon you, so if you veiled the Mothers of the Believers, then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down the verse of hijab."
"6787","7217 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun saying: Humayd narrated to us, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain."
"6788","7218 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah informed me, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to go out at night to al-Manasi, a wide open ground, and Umar used to say to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Veil your women. But the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not do so. Then Sawdah went out one night, and she was a tall woman, so Umar called to her: We have recognized you, O Sawdah, out of eagerness that Allah send down hijab. Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: So Allah sent down hijab."
"6789","7219 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, and he mentioned the same as it with its chain."
"6790","7220 - Rawh narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Anas ibn Malik informed me, he said: I was the most knowledgeable of people about hijab, as it was revealed. The first of it revealed was concerning the building of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Zaynab bint Jahsh; he married her as a bride. He invited the people, they ate food, then left, but a group of them remained with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and stayed long. So the Messenger of Allah rose and went out, and I went out with him until he came to the threshold of the room of Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her). Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thought they had left, so he returned, and I returned with him, until he entered upon Zaynab and they were sitting. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned, and I returned with him, until when he reached the threshold of the room of Aishah, and thought they had left, he returned, and I returned with him, and they had left. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew the curtain between me and him, and hijab was revealed."
"6791","7221 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held a wedding feast when he married Zaynab bint Jahsh, then he went out to the rooms of the Mothers of the Believers. When he returned to his house, he saw two men with whom talk had lingered, so they jumped up hastily. Then he returned until he entered the house and let down the curtain, and the verse of hijab was revealed."
"6792","7222 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from Jarir, from Salim al-Alawi, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I was the servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I used to enter upon him without permission. One day I came to enter, so he said: Stay as you are, for a matter has occurred after you, so do not enter upon us except with permission."
"6793","7223 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Salim al-Alawi, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: When the verse of hijab was revealed, I came to enter as I used to enter. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gently, behind you, O my son."
"6794","7224 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Zaynab bint Jahsh, he invited the people, they ate, then sat talking. He gestured as if preparing to stand, but they did not stand. When he saw that, he stood, and those of the people who stood with him stood, and three sat. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to enter, but the people were sitting. Then they stood and left. So I came and informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they had left, so he came and entered, and the verse of hijab was revealed: O you who believe, do not enter the houses of the Prophet except when permitted, the verse. Abu Jafar said: So the Mothers of the Believers were singled out with hijab, which others were not given like them. If one says: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: And say to the believing women to lower their gazes and guard their private parts and not display their adornment except what appears of it, then He said: And let them not display their adornment except to their husbands or their fathers or their husbands' fathers or their sons or their husbands' sons or their brothers or their brothers' sons or their sisters' sons or their women or what their right hands possess, so He made what their right hands possess like the unmarriageable kin among them. It is said to him: He did not make them so, but He mentioned a group exempted from His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And let them not display their adornment, so He mentioned husbands, fathers, and those mentioned with them, like what He mentioned: Or what their right hands possess. So His joining them is no proof that their rulings are equal, because we have seen a husband may look at his wife at what her father may not look at of her. Then He said: Or what their right hands possess, so joining them with those before them is no proof that their ruling is like their ruling. But what this verse permits slaves of looking at women is only what appears of adornment, namely the face and hands. And His permitting that to slaves, who are not unmarriageable kin, proves that free men who are not unmarriageable kin to women are likewise in that. This meaning is clarified by what is in the hadith of Abd ibn Zamah, of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Sawdah: Veil yourself from him. So he ordered her to veil from him, though he was the son of his father's concubine, and he is either her brother or the son of his father's concubine, thus a slave to her and to the other heirs of her father. So we knew the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not veil her from him because he was her brother, but because he was not her brother, and in that state he was a slave, so his bondage did not make looking at her lawful for him. So this hadith contradicts the hadith of Umm Salamah and opposes it. And the verse we mentioned, according to this holder of the hadith of Sawdah, applies to all women, not the Mothers of the Believers, and the slaves of the Mothers of the Believers were, regarding looking at them, in the ruling of non-kin strangers, not in the ruling of their unmarriageable kin. And everyone between whom and them is unmarriageable kinship is, in our view, in the ruling of unmarriageable kin regarding preventing what we described. Then we returned to consideration to extract thereby a sound view from the two views. We saw that unmarriageable kin may look at the woman to whom he is unmarriageable kin at her face, chest, hair, and below her knees. And we saw a near relative looks at her face and hands only. Then we saw a slave is forbidden, in all their views, to look at a woman's uncovered chest or her legs, whether his bondage is to her or to another. Since in what we mentioned he is like a stranger to her, not like her unmarriageable kin, he is likewise regarding looking at her hair like a stranger, not like her unmarriageable kin. This is the consideration in this chapter, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah, Most High, have mercy on them. And al-Hasan and al-Shabi among the predecessors agreed with them in that."
"6795","7225 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from al-Shabi, and Yunus, from al-Hasan, that they disliked a slave looking at the hair of his mistress."
"6796","Chapter 29 - The chapter of taking the kunyah Abu al-Qasim: is it valid or not? 7226 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from Mundhir al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from Ali, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, if a son is born to me, shall I name him by your name and give him your kunyah? He said: Yes. He said: And it was a concession from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Ali. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that there is no harm in a man taking the kunyah Abu al-Qasim and naming himself Muhammad with that, and they argued in that with what was reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith. And they said: As for what was mentioned that it was a concession, that was not mentioned in the hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor was it mentioned from Ali that it was a concession from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); it is only a saying from one after Ali. It may be as he said, and may be otherwise. The proof that it is otherwise is that in the time of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there was a group named Muhammad with the kunyah Abu al-Qasim, among them Muhammad ibn Talhah, Muhammad ibn al-Ashath, and Muhammad ibn Abi Hudhayfah. Had what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded in the first hadith been specific, others would not have allowed it, and would have denounced its doer, with those present of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then those who held it was specific to Ali said: There has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates what we said."
"6797","7227 - So they mentioned in that what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Aslam narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Waqid narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Mundhir al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If a son is born to you after me, name him by my name and give him my kunyah, and it is for you specifically, apart from people. They said: In this hadith is the specification by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Ali with that, apart from people. It was said to them: This is as you mentioned, had this hadith been established as you reported, but it is not established with us, because Ayyub ibn Waqid does not stand against those who opposed him in this hadith, who reported it from Fitr as we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter. Then those who held it was specific to Ali, after splitting into two groups: one group said: No one should take the kunyah Abu al-Qasim, whether his name is Muhammad or not. The other group said: No one named Muhammad should take the kunyah Abu al-Qasim, but there is no harm for one not named Muhammad to take the kunyah Abu al-Qasim. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates what we said, about the specification by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Ali with that."
"6798","7228 - So they mentioned what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Nakhai, from Abu Zurah ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunyah."
"6799","7229 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as it, except that he said: Name by my name."
"6800","7230 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"6801","7231 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb and Ibn Nafi narrated to us, they said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, (Ha). 7232 - And Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Musa ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunyah, for I am Abu al-Qasim."
"6802","7233 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ashkib al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunyah."
"6803","7234 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Rabiah narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. 7235"
"6804","7235 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah and Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. They said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade taking his kunyah, and permitted taking his name, and that came from him clearly and successively, so that indicates the specificity of what opposed it. Then we returned to the dispute between those who held that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Ibn al-Hanafiyyah was specific to Ali. Among the proof of the group who held that the prohibition mentioned in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah and Jabir is only upon the kunyah specifically, whether the taker is named Muhammad or not, is what has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6805","7236 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Amrah, from his uncle, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade taking his kunyah. So the prohibition in this hadith aims at the kunyah specifically, which indicates that what the prohibition aims at in the reports we mentioned before it is also the kunyah. And what also indicates that is what."
"6806","7237 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunyah; I am Abu al-Qasim, Allah gives, and I distribute."
"6807","7238 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: A boy was born to a man of the Ansar, and he named him Muhammad. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Ansar did well; name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunyah; I am only a distributor, I distribute among you; name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunyah."
"6808","7239 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibn Abi al-Jad, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Name yourselves by my name but do not take my kunyah, for I was made a distributor, I distribute among you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed of the reason for which he forbade taking his kunyah, namely because he distributes among them. So it is established thereby that his intent in the prohibition was toward the kunyah, not the combination of it with the name."
"6809","And they also argued in that with what 7240 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil and Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the market, and a man said: O Abu al-Qasim, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to him, so he, meaning the man, said: I only call that one. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunyah."
"6810","7241 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun saying: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. 7242"
"6811","7242 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. This also indicates that the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is only about taking his kunyah specifically, not combining it with his name. And Ibrahim al-Nakhai and Muhammad ibn Sirin held this view."
"6812","7243 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Waki ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Muhill, he said: I said to Ibrahim: Did they dislike a man taking the kunyah Abu al-Qasim if his name was not Muhammad? He said: Yes. So this Ibrahim also narrates this from those before him, meaning thereby the companions of Abdullah or those above them."
"6813","7244 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunyah. He said: And I saw Muhammad ibn Sirin disliking a man taking the kunyah Abu al-Qasim, whether his name was Muhammad or not."
"6814","And among the proof of those who held that the prohibition in that is only the combination of the kunyah and the name together is what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Khattab al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Hafsah bint al-Bara, from her uncle Ubayd ibn Azib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade combining his name and his kunyah."
"6815","7245 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it."
"6816","7246 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever names himself with my name, let him not use my kunyah, and whoever uses my kunyah, let him not name himself with my name. They said: So it is established through these reports that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited thereof is combining his kunyah with his name. And in the hadith of Jabir is the permissibility of using his kunyah, when one does not take his name along with it. And part of the argument against them, for the people of the other saying, is that it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by his prohibition mentioned in the hadith of al-Bara, Abu Hurayrah and Jabir the combining of the kunyah and the name, and permitted each of them singly, then prohibited using his kunyah after that, and that was an addition to his earlier prohibition thereof. If one says: What makes what you said more worthy than that he prohibited using his kunyah, then prohibited combining his name and his kunyah, which would be permitting part of what his earlier prohibition had covered? It is said to him: Because his prohibition of using his kunyah in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, along with the reports we mentioned with it, must be either before or after what was intended therein of combining the name and the kunyah. If it was after it, then it is an addition to it, not an abrogation of it; and if it was before it, then it was established, then this was narrated after it, so it abrogated it. Since what was intended therein of prohibiting the kunyah may have been abrogated after our knowledge of its establishment, it remains with us upon its earlier basis, and upon its being unabrogated, until we know with certainty that it is abrogated. This is the exposition of this chapter through the meanings of the reports. As for its exposition through rational examination: we have seen that there is no harm in being named with the names of the angels, and likewise the rest of the prophets of Allah, upon them be peace, other than our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - there is no harm in being named with their names, using their kunyahs, and combining the name of each of them with his kunyah. So for this Prophet of ours (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), there is no harm in being named with his name; rational examination upon that basis would be that there is no harm in using his kunyah, and no harm in combining his name and his kunyah. This is the rational examination in this chapter, except that following what has been established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more worthy. And there has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that."
"6817","7247 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah saying: A boy was born to a man of us, and he named him al-Qasim. So I said: We will not give you the kunyah Abu al-Qasim, nor will we gratify your eye. So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: Name your son Abd al-Rahman. So these Ansar disapproved of this man naming his son al-Qasim, lest he be given the kunyah thereby, and by their disapproval thereof they intended the kunyah specifically. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not disapprove of that against them when it reached him. That indicated that the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of using his kunyah [concerns whether] his name is taken along with it or not taken. If one says: In this hadith is what indicates the disapproval of being named al-Qasim. It is said to him: It is possible that that is disapproved as I mentioned, because of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I am only a distributor (qasim) among you. And it is possible that he disapproved of that because they used to give fathers kunyahs with the names of their sons, and most of them would not take a kunyah until a son was born to him, and then he would take his kunyah with his son's name. And the proof of that is what."
"6818","7248 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Hamzah ibn Suhayb, from his father Suhayb who said: Umar said to me: You are an excellent man, O Suhayb, were it not for three traits in you. I said: And what are they, O Commander of the Believers? He said: You have taken a kunyah though no child has been born to you, and there is extravagance in you regarding food, and you have affiliated yourself to the Arabs though you are not of them. I said: As for your saying: You have taken a kunyah though no child has been born to you - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me the kunyah Abu Yahya. And as for your saying: You have affiliated yourself to the Arabs though you are not of them - I am a man of Banu al-Namir ibn Qasit; the Romans took us captive from al-Taif, after I had known my family and my lineage. And as for your saying: There is extravagance in you regarding food - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you are those who feed food. So here Umar disapproved of Suhayb taking a kunyah before a child was born to him, and that indicated that they, or most of them, would not take kunyahs until a child was born to them, and then they would take kunyahs with their sons. So when a son was born to that Ansari and he named him al-Qasim, the Ansar disapproved of that against him, because he only named him thereby in order to be given the kunyah thereby, so they refused that and disapproved of it against him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praised them for that. And what indicated that also is what."
"6819","7249 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki informed him, from Jabir ibn Abdillah who said: A boy was born to a man of us, and he named him al-Qasim and took the kunyah thereby, but the Ansar refused to give him the kunyah thereby. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: The Ansar did well; take my name, but do not take my kunyah. So in this hadith is what indicated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only changed the name of that boy because his father had taken the kunyah thereby, so he changed it to a name with which it is permissible for his father to take the kunyah. And in it is what indicates that the prohibition was intended only for the kunyah specifically, not for combining it with the name, and Allah Almighty knows best."
"6820","Chapter 30: Greeting the people of disbelief with salam. 7250 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Rumi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Thawr narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Usamah ibn Zayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a gathering in which there was a mixture of Muslims, Jews, and polytheists of the idol-worshippers, and he greeted them with salam. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that there is no harm in initiating the people of disbelief with salam, and they argued for that with this hadith. Others differed with them in that, and disapproved of initiating them with salam, and said: There is no harm in returning it to them when they greet. And they argued for that with what."
"6821","7251 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik and Abu Bakr (meaning Ibn Ayyash) narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not initiate them with salam - meaning: the Jews and the Christians."
"6822","7252 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Suhayl, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6823","7253 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6824","7254 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Suhayl, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6825","7255 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Marthad ibn Abdillah al-Yazani, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Juhani who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am riding tomorrow to the Jews, so do not initiate them, and when they greet you, say: And upon you."
"6826","7256 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he said: Do not initiate them with salam."
"6827","7257 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Marthad ibn Abdillah al-Yazani, from Abu Basrah al-Ghifari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he did not say: with salam."
"6828","7258 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, that he heard Abu Basrah al-Ghifari saying: He heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I am riding to the Jews; when you come to them and they greet you, say: And upon you."
"6829","7259 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in these reports is the prohibition of initiating the Jews and Christians with salam, from the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in the first hadith is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted them, in the saying of Usamah. So it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by his salam those of them who were Muslims, and did not intend the Jews, nor the Christians, nor the idol-worshippers, so that these reports do not contradict one another, and what we described is possible. For it is possible for a man to greet a group while intending some of them. And it may be that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted them all, because that was at a time when he had been commanded not to dispute with them except in the best manner, and salam was part of that; then he was commanded to fight them and dissociate from them, so that abrogated what had preceded of his greeting them."
"6830","7260 - So we examined that, and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri who said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, that Usamah ibn Zayd informed him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode upon a donkey, upon it a pack-saddle over a velvet cloth, and he took Usamah ibn Zayd behind him, visiting Sad ibn Ubadah among Banu al-Harith ibn Khazraj, before the battle of Badr. He went until he passed a gathering in which was Abdillah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, at that time before Abdillah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul accepted Islam, and in the gathering was a mixture of Muslims, polytheist idol-worshippers, and Jews, and in the gathering was Abdillah ibn Rawahah. When the dust of the beast covered the gathering, Ibn Ubayy ibn Salul covered his nose with his cloak, then said: Do not make dust over us. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted them, then stopped and dismounted, and called them to Allah Almighty and recited the Quran to them. Abdillah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul said: O man, what you say is good, if it is true, so do not harm us with it in our gatherings; return to your place, and whoever comes to you, relate to him. Abdillah ibn Rawahah said: Rather, O Messenger of Allah, come to us with it in our gatherings, for we love that. So the Muslims, polytheists and Jews reviled one another until they almost fought, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept calming them until they settled, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mounted his beast and went until he entered upon Sad ibn Ubadah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Sad, did you not hear what Abu Hubab says? Meaning Ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, he said such and such. Sad said: O Messenger of Allah, pardon him and overlook; by Him who sent down the Book upon you, Allah brought you the truth that was revealed to you, and the people of this oasis had agreed to crown him and wrap him with the turban, but when Allah Almighty repelled that with the truth He gave you, he choked with it, so he did what you saw, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pardoned him. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions used to pardon the polytheists and the People of the Book, and be patient over harm, until Allah Almighty said: You will surely hear from those who were given the Book before you and from those who associate partners much harm, and if you are patient and fear Allah, that is of matters of resolve. And Allah Almighty said: Many of the People of the Book wish to turn you back after your faith to disbelievers, out of envy from themselves - the verse. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to interpret pardon as Allah Almighty commanded him, until Allah gave permission concerning them. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fought at Badr, and Allah Almighty killed thereby those He killed of the chiefs of the disbelievers of Quraysh, Ibn Ubayy ibn Salul and those with him of the polytheist idol-worshippers said: This is a matter that has turned, so they pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon Islam, and they accepted Islam. So in this hadith is that the greeting of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon them was at the time when Allah commanded him to pardon them and overlook, and to leave disputing them except in the best manner; then Allah abrogated that and commanded him to fight them, so greeting them was abrogated along with that, and his saying was established: Do not initiate the Jews and Christians with salam, and whoever of them greets you, say: And upon you, until you return upon him what he said. And they were prohibited from adding to that."
"6831","7261 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Zadawayh, from Anas ibn Malik who said: We were prohibited from adding for the People of the Book over: And upon you. So we adopt this, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy upon them."
"6832","Book of Additions, Chapter 1: The prayer of the two Eids, how is takbir therein? 7262 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir in the two Eids twelve takbirs; seven in the first, and five in the last, apart from the two takbirs of prayer. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that takbir in the prayer of the two Eids is likewise, and they argued for that with this hadith."
"6833","And with what: 7263 - Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, from Abu Waqid al-Laythi, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people on the day of Fitr and Adha, and said takbir in the first seven, and recited: Qaf, by the Glorious Quran, and in the second five, and recited: The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split."
"6834","7264 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say takbir in the two Eids seven and five, apart from the two takbirs of bowing."
"6835","7265 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6836","7266 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6837","7267 - Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Harmalah narrated to us, from Ibn Wahb, from Ibn Lahiah, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6838","7268 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abdus al-Attar narrated to us, from al-Faraj ibn Fadalah, from Abdillah ibn Amir al-Aslami, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said concerning the takbir of the two Eids: in the first rakah seven, and in the second five takbirs."
"6839","7269 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Kathir ibn Abdillah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to me, narrating to me from his father, from his grandfather who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say takbir in Adha seven, and five in Fitr, the like of that. They said: And that has also been narrated from more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So they mentioned what:"
"6840","7270 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Nafi who said: I witnessed Adha and Fitr with Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said takbir in the first seven takbirs before the recitation, and in the last five takbirs before the recitation."
"6841","7271 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Malik and Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Nafi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it. They said: So with these reports we say, and to them we go. Others differed with them in that and said: Rather takbir in the two Eids is nine takbirs; five in the first, and four in the last, joining the two recitations. And part of the argument for them against the people of the first saying, concerning what they adduced against them of the reports we mentioned, is that the hadith of Abdillah ibn Umar only turns upon Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman, and he is not, in their view, one whose narration is adduced as proof. Then it is also from Amr ibn Shuayb from his father from his grandfather, and that too, in their view, is not hearing. So how do they argue against their opponent with what, had he argued against them with it, they would not have allowed him? As for the hadith of Ibn Lahiah, it is clearly confused: once he narrates from Uqayl, once from Khalid ibn Yazid from Ibn Shihab, once from Khalid ibn Yazid from Uqayl from Ibn Shihab, and once from Abu al-Aswad from Urwah from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and Abu Waqid, may Allah be pleased with him - and we mentioned all that in this chapter. Moreover, their doctrine concerning Ibn Lahiah is what we have explained in more than one place of this book. As for the hadith of Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, it only turns upon what they narrated from Abdillah ibn Amir, and he is weak in their view. The origin of this hadith is only from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from himself. 7272"
"6842","7272 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to me, from Nafi ibn Abi Nuaym, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it, and he did not raise it - so this is the origin of the hadith. As for the hadith of Kathir ibn Abdillah, it is only from his letter to Ibn Wahb, and they do not make what was heard from him a proof, so how what was not heard from him? So when it was negated that there be in these reports anything indicating the manner of takbir in the two Eids, because of the feebleness and fall we explained, we looked into other than them: is there therein anything indicating something of that? And then"
"6843","Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman and Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Hamzah who said: 7273 - al-Wadin ibn Ata narrated to me, that al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to him, he said: One of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer on a day of Eid, and said takbir four and four, then turned to us with his face when he finished, and said: Do not forget, like the takbir of funerals, and he gestured with his fingers, and closed his thumb. So this is a hadith with a fair isnad. And Abdillah ibn Yusuf, Yahya ibn Hamzah, al-Wadin, and al-Qasim are all people of narration known for sound narration, not like those from whom we narrated the first reports. So if this chapter is taken by way of soundness of isnad, then this is more worthy to be taken than what opposed it. Except that he mentioned therein that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir in each rakah four, and informed them that that is like the takbir of funerals. So it may be that the four are apart from the opening takbir, and that would agree with the saying of those for whose saying we adduced this hadith. And it may be that that is upon four, with the opening takbir, and would oppose their saying. So we looked into what was narrated of the reports in this chapter, besides this report also."
"6844","7274 - And Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Jawzajani narrated to us, he said: Ghassan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban narrated to us, from his father, that he heard Makhul saying: Abu Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me, that Said ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, invited Abu Musa al-Ashari and Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with them both, and asked them: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say takbir in Adha and Fitr? Abu Musa said: Four, like his takbir over funerals, and Hudhayfah confirmed him. Abu Musa said: Likewise I used to say takbir for the people of Basrah, when I was governor over them. So there was not in this either any addition over what is in the first hadith. So we looked into that also."
"6845","7275 - And Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Wasiti narrated to us, from al-Numan ibn al-Mundhir, from Makhul who said: The messenger of Hudhayfah and Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say takbir in the two Eids four and four, apart from the opening takbir. So this hadith clarified that the opening takbir is outside the mentioned takbirs in the hadith of al-Jawzajani, and in the hadith of Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman and Yahya ibn Uthman. So this is what is established with us concerning takbir in the two Eids from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); we know of nothing narrated from him of what is as established as it, opposing anything of that. As for what they adduced of the hadith of Nafi from Abu Hurayrah, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, then there has been narrated from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the contrary of that, among them Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6846","7276 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to say takbir in Nahr five takbirs; three in the first, and two in the second, not joining the two recitations. So thus Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say takbir in Nahr, and he used to say takbir in Fitr contrary to that."
"6847","7277 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to say takbir on the day of Fitr eleven takbirs: opening with one takbir, then reciting, then saying five takbirs, bowing with one of them, then standing and reciting, then saying five takbirs, bowing with one of them. Then there was mentioned from him, concerning what he used to say in Adha, the like of what Abu Bakrah mentioned. So thus Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say takbir in Fitr. And what Yahya mentioned in this hadith of his indicated that the abandonment by Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, of joining the two recitations was only because he used to say part of the takbir he said in the first rakah before the recitation, and part after the recitation, and that he used to begin with recitation in the second rakah, before the takbir he said therein. And there has also been narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the contrary of that."
"6848","7278 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: al-Abbas ibn Talib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Amir, that Umar and Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, their view agreed in the takbir of the two Eids upon nine takbirs; five in the first, and four in the last, joining the two recitations. And there has also been narrated the contrary of that from Abdillah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"6849","7279 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Qatadah and Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn al-Harith, that he prayed behind Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in Eid, and he said four takbirs, then recited, then said takbir and bowed, then stood in the second and recited, then said three takbirs, then said takbir and bowed."
"6850","7280 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha informed us, from Abdillah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it. And there has also been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, what opposes this saying, and the saying of the people of the first saying."
"6851","7281 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Amr narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to say takbir on the day of Fitr thirteen takbirs, seven in the first before the recitation, and six in the last after the recitation."
"6852","7282 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik and Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it, and he did not mention the recitation."
"6853","And there has also been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in that, of his saying, what 7283 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: Whoever wishes says seven takbirs, and whoever wishes says nine, and eleven and thirteen. So here Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both - Ikrimah narrated from him what we mentioned - and that indicated that he said takbir upon what each of Abdillah ibn al-Harith and Ata narrated from him, and he may say takbir upon what the other party narrated from him. And they differed from him concerning the place of recitation, and each of them narrated from him what we mentioned in his hadith. So it may be that the ruling in that, in his view, was to do whichever of these two one wishes. And it may be that the ruling, in his view, for one who said nine was to join the two recitations, and for one who said thirteen was to separate the two recitations. And there has also been narrated the contrary of that from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6854","7284 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ibrahim ibn Abdillah ibn Qays, from his father, that Said ibn al-As invited them on a day of Eid, and invited al-Ashari and Ibn Masud and Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with them. He said: Today is your Eid, so how shall I pray? Hudhayfah said: Ask al-Ashari, and al-Ashari said: Ask Abdillah. Abdillah said: You say takbir, and he mentioned the hadith: he says a takbir, opening the prayer with it, then says three takbirs after it, then recites, then says a takbir with which he bows, then prostrates, then stands and recites, then says three takbirs, then says a takbir with which he bows."
"6855","7285 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdillah ibn Abi Musa, from Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning takbir on the day of Eid, and he mentioned the like of that. 7286"
"6856","7286 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah ibn Qays who said: al-Walid ibn Uqbah ibn Abi Muayt came to Ibn Masud, Hudhayfah and al-Ashari, may Allah be pleased with them, and said: Eid is tomorrow, so how is the takbir? Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said, and mentioned the like of that, and added: al-Ashari and Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Abu Abd al-Rahman spoke the truth. So here Hudhayfah and Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with them both, agreed with Abdillah upon what he held of takbir and the manner of Eid prayer. And there has also been narrated the contrary of that from Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr."
"6857","7287 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj who said: Yusuf ibn Mahak informed me, that Ibn al-Zubayr would not say takbir except four, apart from two takbirs for the two rakahs - hearing that from him is an allegation. So it may be that the four he used to say were in the first rakah apart from the opening takbir, and what was done thereof would agree with what Ibn Masud, Hudhayfah and Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with them, held. And it may be that the opening takbir is included in them, and that would oppose their doctrine. And it is more worthy for us to carry it upon what agreed with their saying, not upon what opposed it. And there has also been narrated the contrary of that from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6858","7288 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: al-Ashath narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Nine takbirs; five in the first, and four in the last, with the takbir of prayer."
"6859","7289 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Anas ibn Malik informed us, from his grandfather Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When he was at his dwelling in al-Taff and did not attend Eid at his city, he would gather his freedmen and his children, then order his freedman Abdillah ibn Abi Utbah, and he would pray with them like the prayer of the city people. Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abdillah ibn al-Harith from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, which we mentioned in this chapter, exactly. And there has been narrated from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, the contrary of that also."
"6860","7290 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, and Masruq and Said ibn al-Musayyib, that they said: Ten takbirs with the takbir of prayer, and Qatadah holds this. And others of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed that."
"6861","7291 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Makhul who said: One whom Said ibn al-As sent narrated to me, and four of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreed upon eight takbirs. So this hadith is the hadith we narrated earlier in this chapter, and among the four are Abu Musa and Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with them both, and they confirmed Abu Abd al-Rahman in what he gave fatwa with to al-Walid ibn Uqbah, and in what he gave fatwa with that the opening takbir is apart from these eight takbirs. So it is established thereby that the takbirs in this hadith, and in the hadith of al-Jawzajani, are apart from the opening takbir. This is what was narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the takbir of the two Eids. And there has been narrated from their followers disagreement therein. Of what was narrated from them therein is what."
"6862","7292 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Attab ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Khusayf, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, may Allah have mercy upon him, used to say seven and five takbirs. So the people of the first saying said: Here Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz agreed our doctrine with his doctrine. It was said to them: There has been narrated from most of the followers the contrary of this."
"6863","7293 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that Masruq ibn al-Ajda, may Allah have mercy upon him, used to say takbir in the two Eids nine takbirs."
"6864","7294 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Mansur narrating, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad and Masruq, that they used to say takbir in the two Eids nine takbirs."
"6865","7295 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: al-Ashath narrated to us, from al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy upon him, who said: Nine takbirs; five in the first, and four in the last, with the takbir of prayer."
"6866","7296 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, may Allah have mercy upon him, who said: Nine takbirs."
"6867","7297 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Hamzah Abu Umarah saying: I heard al-Shabi, may Allah have mercy upon him, saying: Three, three, apart from the takbir of prayer."
"6868","7298 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad - and he is Ibn Sirin - narrated to us concerning the takbir of the two Eids, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of the takbir of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, and he also agreed with him upon joining the two recitations."
"6869","7299 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad, with the like of it. So most of those we narrated from of the followers agree in saying with the saying of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him. And when there was this disagreement concerning takbir in the prayer of the two Eids, we wanted to examine that to extract from these sayings of theirs a sound saying. So we examined that, and it was not narrated from any of them that he differentiated between prayer in Fitr and Adha other than Ali, may Allah be pleased with him; and the prayer of Fitr and the prayer of Nahr are two Eid prayers done for one meaning, and they are equal in their bowing and prostration. So rational examination was that they be alike, with no difference between one and the other in the rest of their ruling. So there is established, by what we mentioned, the equalizing of the two prayers on the day of Nahr and the day of Fitr. Then we looked into the number of takbir in them, and we saw the rest of prayers free of this takbir, and we saw that the prayer of the two Eids is agreed to have additional takbirs over other prayers. So rational examination was not to add in prayer for the two Eids over what is in the rest of prayers other than them, except what was agreed upon adding; and all agreed upon adding the nine takbirs upon what Ibn Masud, Hudhayfah, Ibn Abbas, Abu Musa and those we heard with them, may Allah be pleased with them, held. And they differed in adding over that, so we added in this prayer what was agreed upon adding therein, and negated from it what was not agreed upon adding therein. So there is established thereby what the people of this saying held. Then we looked into the place of recitation therein: those who held that it is in the first rakah after takbir, and in the second likewise, said: You have agreed with us that recitation in the first rakah is delayed after takbir, so rational examination is that it be likewise in the second. And part of the argument against them, for the people of the other saying, is that takbir is a remembrance done in prayer, other than recitation. So we looked into the place of remembrance in the first rakah of prayer, and in the second rakah, where is its place? We found the first rakah has the opening and seeking refuge, as we narrated in other than this place of this book from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and from those we narrated from of his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, and that was at the beginning of prayer before recitation. So there is established thereby that likewise the place of takbir in the Eid prayer in the first rakah is that place thereof. And we found qunut in witr done in the last rakah of witr prayer, and all agreed that it is after recitation, and that recitation precedes it; they only differed in preceding bowing over it, and preceding it over bowing. As for delaying it after recitation, no. So there is established thereby that the place of takbir in the last rakah of Eid prayer is after recitation, equal to the place of the rest of remembrance in prayers; and the place of all wherein they differed in its place is like the place of what is agreed upon its place. And all we clarified in this chapter is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"6870","Chapter 2: Ruling of the woman in her wealth. 7300 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Abdillah ibn Yahya al-Ansari, from his father, from his grandfather, that his grandmother came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with her jewelry, and said: I have given this in charity. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No disposal by a woman in her wealth is permissible except with her husband's permission; have you sought your husband's permission? She said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent, and said: Have you permitted your wife to give this jewelry of hers in charity? He said: Yes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted it from her. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to this hadith, and said: It is not permissible for a woman to give anything of her wealth, nor to give it in charity, without her husband's permission. Others differed with them in that, and permitted all her disposal in her wealth, and made her in her wealth like her husband in his wealth. And they argued for that with the saying of Allah Almighty: Give women their dowries graciously, and if they willingly give you part of it, eat it wholesome and pleasant. So Allah permitted the husband what his wife's self is good with for him. And with His saying Almighty: If you divorce them before touching them while you have appointed for them an appointment, then half of what you appointed, except that they remit. So He permitted their remission of their wealth, after her husband divorces her, without consultation of anyone. So that indicated the permissibility of the woman's disposal in her wealth, and that she is in her wealth like the man in his wealth. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what agrees with this meaning also. It is what we narrated from him in the Book of Zakah concerning the wife of Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, when she took her jewelry to go with it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to give it in charity. Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Come, give it in charity upon me. She said: No, until I seek the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sought his permission in that, and he said: Give it in charity upon him, and upon the orphans in his care; they are a place for it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted her to give her jewelry in charity upon her husband and upon his orphans, and did not command her to consult him in what she gave upon his orphans. And in this hadith also is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exhorted the women and said: Give charity, and did not mention therein the matter of their husbands. So that indicated that they may give in charity what they wish of their wealth without their husbands' command."
"6871","7301 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh and Abu al-Walid narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ayyub narrating to Ata, he said: I bear witness concerning Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both. Or he narrated it from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I bear witness upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he went out on a day of Fitr, and prayed, then preached, then came to the women, and commanded them to give charity."
"6872","7302 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abbas who said: I said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: Did you witness Eid with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, and were it not for my place from him I would not have witnessed it, due to my smallness. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on the day of Eid, and prayed, then preached, then came to the women with Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, and exhorted them. So the woman began reaching with her hand to her neck, and the woman reaching with her hand to her ear, and handing it to Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, and Bilal putting it in his garment, then he went with it with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his house."
"6873","7303 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Muslim narrated to me, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I witnessed prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them - all of them pray it before the sermon, then preach after. He said: And the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended, and it is as if I look at him seating the man with his hand, then he came cleaving them until he came to the women, and with him Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: O Prophet, when the believing women come to you pledging that they will not associate anything with Allah, to His saying: Forgiving, Merciful. When he finished he said: Are you upon that? One woman, none other answered him, said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then give charity. So Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, spread his garment, then said to them: Cast, and they began casting rings and signet-rings into the garment of Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6874","7304 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ata informed me, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I heard him saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the day of Fitr, and began with prayer before the sermon, then preached to the people. When the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he stood and came to the women, and reminded them while leaning upon Bilal, and Bilal spreading his garment, and the women began casting their charities into it."
"6875","7305 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Hisham al-Halabi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Zayd ibn Rufay, from Hizam ibn Hakim ibn Hizam, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached to the women one day, and commanded them with fear of Allah Almighty, and obedience to their husbands, and to give charity. So here the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the women with charities, and accepted them from them, and did not wait therein for the view of their husbands. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that also."
"6876","7306 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to us, from Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I heard Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: I freed a slave-girl in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Had you given her to your Bedouin sister, it would have been greater for your reward."
"6877","7307 - Rabi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah, from Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it. So had the disposal of a woman in her wealth without her husband's permission been impermissible, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have rejected her manumission, and directed the slave-girl to what is better than manumission. So how is it permissible for anyone to abandon two verses of the Book of Allah Almighty, and established sunnahs from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreed upon the soundness of their coming, for an anomalous hadith the like of which is not established? Then rational examination after that indicates what we mentioned: we saw they do not differ concerning the woman, in her bequests from a third of her wealth, that they are valid from her third, like the bequests of men, and her husband has over her therein no way nor command, and with that the Mighty Book spoke. Allah Almighty said: And for you is half of what your wives leave if they have no child, and if they have a child then for you a fourth of what they leave, after a bequest they bequeath or a debt. So when her bequests in a third of her wealth are valid after her death, her acts in her wealth in her life are more valid than that. So with this we take, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"6878","Chapter 3: What the praying person does after raising from the last prostration of the first rakah. 7308 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to me, from Abu Qilabah, from Malik ibn al-Huwayrith, that he used to say to his companions: Shall I not show you how the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was? And that was at other than prayer time. So he stood, and made the standing firm, then bowed, and made the bowing firm, then raised his head and stood upright a while, then prostrated, then raised his head and settled in sitting, then waited a while, then prostrated. Abu Qilabah said: So he prayed like the prayer of this shaykh of ours (meaning Amr ibn Salamah, may Allah be pleased with him). He said: So I saw Amr ibn Salamah doing something I do not see you doing: when he raised his head from the first and third prostration in which there is no sitting, he sat upright, then stood."
"6879","7309 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid informed us, from Abu Qilabah who said: Malik ibn al-Huwayrith, may Allah be pleased with him, informed us, that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): when he was in an odd part of his prayer he would not rise until he sat upright. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that when a man raises his head from the second prostration of the first and third rakah, he sits until he is settled sitting, then stands after, and they argued for that with this hadith. Others differed with them in that, and said: Rather he stands from it, and does not wait to sit upright."
"6880","7310 - And they argued for that with what more than one of our companions, may Allah have mercy upon them, narrated to me - among them Ali ibn Said ibn Bashir al-Razi, who said: Abu Hammam al-Walid ibn Shuja al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn al-Kufi ibn al-Harr narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Abdillah ibn Malik narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, may Allah be pleased with him, one of Banu Malik from Ayyash - or Abbas ibn Sahl al-Saidi - and he was in a gathering in which was his father, and he was of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in the gathering were Abu Hurayrah, Abu Usayd, Abu Humayd al-Saidi and the Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them, that they recalled prayer. Abu Humayd said: I am the most knowledgeable of you of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I followed that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: Then show us. So he stood praying while they looked, and said takbir and raised his hands at the first takbir, then he mentioned a long hadith, in which he mentioned: when he raised his head from the second prostration of the first rakah he stood and did not sit cross-legged. So when this hadith came as we mentioned, opposing the first hadith, it may be that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in the first hadith was due to an ailment in him, so he sat because of it, not because that is of the sunnah of prayer - just as Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to sit cross-legged in prayer, and when asked about that said: My legs do not carry me. So likewise it may be that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did of that sitting was due to an ailment that befell him, so that it does not contradict what was narrated from him in the other hadith, nor oppose it. And this is more worthy for us than carrying what was narrated from him upon contradiction and opposition. And in the hadith of Abu Humayd also is Abu Humayd's narration of what was narrated in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and none of them disapproved that against him. So that indicated that what is with them therein does not oppose what he narrated to them. And in the hadith of Malik ibn al-Huwayrith, may Allah be pleased with him, in Ayyub's speech, is that what Amr ibn Salamah used to do thereof, the people were not seen doing it - and he had seen a group of the followers. So that is a proof in repelling what was narrated from Abu Qilabah from Malik that it be a sunnah. Then rational examination after this agrees with what Abu Humayd, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: we saw that when a man moves in his prayer from a state to a state, he begins remembrance. Of that: we saw him, when intending bowing, say takbir and fall bowing; when raising his head from bowing, say: Allah hears him who praises Him; when falling from standing to prostration, say: Allah is Greatest; when raising his head from prostration, say: Allah is Greatest; when returning to prostration, do that also; and when raising his head he does not say takbir after raising his head until he stands upright, other than one takbir. So that indicated that there is no sitting between his prostration and his standing. Had there been sitting between them, his takbir after raising his head from prostration would need to be for entering that sitting, and he would need another takbir when rising for standing. Since he was not commanded with that, it is established that there is no sitting between rising from the last prostration and standing to the rakah after it, so that its ruling and the ruling of the rest of prayers agree, not differ. So with this we take, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"6881","Chapter 4: What is due to the slave upon his master of clothing and food. 7311 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 7312 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Jafar narrated to us, both saying: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Mujahid al-Madani, Abu Hazrah, narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn al-Walid ibn Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I and my father went out seeking this knowledge in this quarter of the Ansar, before they perished. The first we met was Abu al-Yasar, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with him was a slave of his, and upon him was a cloak and a Maafiri garment, and upon his slave was a cloak and a Maafiri garment. He said: So I said to him: O uncle, had you taken your slave's cloak and given him your Maafiri, and taken his Maafiri and given him your cloak, there would be upon you a suit, and upon him a suit. He said: So he wiped my head and said: O Allah, bless him. Then he said: O son of my brother, these two eyes of mine saw, these two ears of mine heard, and my heart retained from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while he says: Feed them of what you eat, and clothe them of what you wear; and my giving him of the goods of this world is dearer to me than his taking from my good deeds on the Day of Resurrection."
"6882","7313 - Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Marur ibn Suwayd who said: We went out as pilgrims - or performing Umrah - and met Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, at al-Rabadhah, and upon him was a cloak, and upon his slave a like cloak. So we said to him: O Abu Dharr, had you taken this cloak to your cloak, it would be a suit, and clothed him with another cloak. Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Your brothers, Allah Almighty placed them under your hands; whoever has his brother under his hand, let him feed him of what he eats, clothe him of what he wears, and not burden him with what overcomes him; if he burdens him with what overcomes him, let him help him. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that a man must equalize between his slave and himself in food and clothing. And they argued for that with what we narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with what we narrated of the doctrine of Abu al-Yasar and Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with them both, which we mentioned therein. Others differed with them in that, and said: What is due to the slave upon his master is his food and his clothing, not other than that of what a man widens upon himself. And they argued for that with what."
"6883","7314 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdillah ibn al-Ashajj, from Ajlan Abu Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For the slave is his food and his clothing, and he is not burdened of work except what he can bear. They said: So this is what is due to the slave upon his master. And the worthiest thing for us, since this was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is to carry what we narrated before it in this chapter upon what agrees with it, as we find a way to that. So the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Feed them of what you eat, and clothe them of what you wear, may mean bread and condiment, and garments of linen and cotton; when they share their masters therein, they have eaten of what they eat, and worn of what they wear, and that would agree with the meaning of the hadith of Abu Hurayrah. Equalization would only be obligatory had he said: Feed them the like of what you eat, and clothe them the like of what you wear. Had he said this, it would not be permissible for masters to prefer their slaves in food or clothing; but he only said: Feed them of what you eat, and clothe them of what you wear. So there was not therein the obligation of equalization between them in clothing and food; therein is only the obligation of clothing of what they wear, and the obligation of food of what they eat, though they be unequal therein. And what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also indicated that."
"6884","7315 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you's servant suffices him his food, its heat and smoke, let him seat him and eat with him; if he refuses, let him take a morsel, mix it, then feed it to him."
"6885","7316 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, from Shubah, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you's servant brings his food, if he does not seat him with him, let him give him a meal or two - or said: a morsel or two - for he undertook its heat and preparation. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) widened upon the master to feed his slave of his food, whose making his slave undertook for him, one morsel, then keep for himself what remains of that food after that morsel. So that indicated that the meaning of what he intended by his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Feed them of what you eat, is that he did not intend equalization; likewise the meaning of his saying: And clothe them of what you wear. As for what Abu al-Yasar did, it was out of compassion and fear, not other than that. And this upon which we corrected the meanings of these reports is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"6886","Chapter 5: Reciting poetry in mosques. 7317 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited reciting poems in the mosque, selling goods therein, and gathering in circles therein before prayer. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the disapproval of reciting poetry in mosques, and argued for that with this hadith. Others differed with them in that, and saw no harm in reciting poetry in the mosque when that poetry is of what there is no harm in narrating and reciting outside the mosque. And they argued for that with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than this place, that he placed for Hassan a pulpit in the mosque upon which he recited poetry, and with what we narrated along with that of the hadith of Hassan, may Allah be pleased with him, when Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by him while he recited poetry in the mosque and rebuked him. Hassan, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: I used to recite poetry therein for one better than you - and that in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and none of them disapproved that against him, nor did Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, disapprove it against him either. And the hadith of Yunus with which we began in the first of this chapter may be that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended thereby the poetry he prohibited to be recited in the mosque: the poetry with which Quraysh lampooned him. And it may be of the poetry in which women are defamed, and wealth is disparaged, as we mentioned in the chapter of narrating poetry, of the answer of the Ansar - of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - to Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, thereby, when he disapproved against them reciting poetry around the Kabah. And it may also be that he intended thereby poetry that overcomes the mosque, until all in it or most in it are occupied thereby - like what Ibn Aishah and Abu Ubayd interpreted the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): For one of you's belly to be filled with pus until it overflows is better for him than to be filled with poetry, as we mentioned that from them in other than this place. So the poetry prohibited in this hadith would be a specific kind of poetry: that in which is a meaning of these three meanings we mentioned, so that it does not contradict what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of its permissibility, and what his Companions did after him. If one says: If it is as you mentioned, why did he aim at the mosque? What you mentioned of what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was lampooned with, and what women were defamed and wealth disparaged in, is disapproved outside the mosque; had it been as you mentioned, mentioning the mosque would have no meaning? It is said to him: Speech often runs with mention of a meaning, yet that meaning is not thereby made specific to the ruling that ran in that mention. Of that is the saying of Allah Almighty: Your stepdaughters in your care of your wives with whom you consummated; if you did not consummate with them, no sin upon you. So He mentioned the stepdaughter who was in her stepfather's care, yet that was not specific to her, that she be under that ruling because she was in his care, excluding her when she was not in his care. Do you not see that had she been older than him, she is prohibited to him, as her prohibition had she been small in his care? And He Almighty also said concerning game: Whoever of you kills it deliberately, recompense like what he killed of cattle. The scholars agreed - except who diverged of them - that his killing it forgetfully is likewise in obligation of recompense. So His mentioning what we mentioned of these two verses did not necessitate specificity of ruling. Likewise what we narrated of his mentioning the mosque in poetry prohibited to narrate: there is no proof therein of the mosque's specificity with that. Likewise what was prohibited of selling in the mosque is selling that pervades it or overcomes it until it is like the market; that is disapproved. As for other than that, no. We narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates the permissibility of work that is not of devotions in the mosque."
"6887","7318 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O company of Quraysh, Allah will surely send upon you a man, with whom Allah tested faith, striking your necks upon religion. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Am I he, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Am I he, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No, but he is the mender of the sandal in the mosque. He said: His sandal had been cast to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, mending it. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prohibit Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from mending the sandal in the mosque, and that had people gathered until they fill the mosque with mending sandals, that would be disapproved. Since what of this does not fill the mosque is not disapproved, and what fills it or overcomes it is disapproved, so it is in selling, reciting poetry, and gathering in circles therein before prayer: what of that fills it is disapproved, and what does not fill it nor overcome it is not disapproved, and Allah knows best the right."
"6888","Chapter 6: Buying the absent thing. 7319 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus ibn al-Qasim al-Yamami narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited mulamasah and munabadhah."
"6889","7320 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6890","7321 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Amir ibn Sad, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6891","7322 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6892","7323 - Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Hassan ibn Ghalib and Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Qari narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that when a man buys what he has not seen, his buying it is impermissible, and in that they went to an interpretation they interpreted in this hadith. They said: Mulamasah is what its buyer touches with his hand without looking at it with his eye. They said: Munabadhah is also of this meaning: a man saying to a man: Cast to me your garment, and I cast to you my garment, upon each of them being sold to his companion without looking of each of the two buyers at his companion's garment. And of those who went to this interpretation is Malik ibn Anas, may Allah have mercy upon him. Others differed with them in that, and said: Whoever buys a thing absent from him, the sale is permissible, and he has therein the option of seeing: if he wishes he takes it, and if he wishes he leaves it. And in the interpretation of the first hadith they went to that the prohibited mulamasah therein is: a sale the people of ignorance used to trade among themselves - two men would bargain over a garment, and when the bargainer touched it, he was thereby its buyer, and delivering it to him became obligatory upon its owner. Likewise munabadhah: they would also talk in a garment and the like, then its owner would throw it to the one he talked with. So that would be a sale from him to him of his garment, and he would have no rescinding it after that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited that, and made the ruling in sales that they are only obligatory with mutually agreed contracts, and said: The two sellers are with option while they have not separated. So he made the casting of one of them to his companion the garment before separating him not cutting his option. Then people differed after that in the manner of that separation, as we mentioned of that in its place of this book. And of those who went to this interpretation is Abu Hanifah, may Allah be pleased with him. When they differed in that, we wanted to look into other than this hadith of the hadiths: is there therein what indicates one of the two sayings we mentioned. So we looked, and"
"6893","7324 - And Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited selling grapes until they blacken, and selling grain until it hardens. So that indicated the permissibility of its sale after it hardens while in its ear; had that not been so he would have said: Until it hardens and is free of its ear. When he made the limit in the prohibited sale its hardening and dryness, that indicated that sale after that is contrary to what it was at the start. When the sale of grain hidden in the ear became permissible, this indicated the permissibility of selling what the two traders do not see, when they return with it to something known, as they return from sold wheat hidden in the ear to known wheat. And the worthiest thing for us in the like of this, since we stood upon the interpretation of this hadith, and the other hadith may agree with it or oppose it, is to carry it upon agreeing with it, not upon opposing it."
"6894","7324 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, concerning the interpretation of mulamasah and munabadhah. He said: The people used to trade goods, not looking at them, nor being informed of them. And munabadhah: that the people cast goods to one another, not looking at them, nor being informed of them; this is of the doors of gambling. 7325 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Rabiah, who said: This was of the doors of gambling, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited it. So here al-Zuhri - and he is one from whom this hadith was narrated - permitted a man to buy what he was informed of, though he had not seen it. So therein is proof of the permissibility of buying the absent. One of those who went to the interpretation we mentioned first in this chapter said: Whence did you permit selling the absent while it is unknown? It was said to him: It is not unknown in itself; whenever one returns to it, one returns to something known - like selling wheat in its ear, returned from to known wheat. The ignorance in this is only the ignorance of seller and buyer; as for the sold thing in itself, it is not unknown. The unknown whose sale is impermissible is the unknown in itself that does not return to something known, like some unspecified food a man sells to a man. That part is unknown, and not returned to something known, so contract upon that is impermissible. And we found sale validly contracted upon specific food upon its being so many qafiz, while seller and buyer do not know its true measure. Of the rights of sale is the obligation of measuring for the buyer upon the seller, and their ignorance thereof does not necessitate the sale falling upon an unknown measure, when they return from that to a known measure. Likewise the absent food when sold, with buyer and seller ignorant of it: their ignorance thereof does not necessitate the contract falling upon something unknown, when they return from it to known food. This is the rational examination in this chapter, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all. And we narrated earlier in this book that Uthman and Talhah, may Allah be pleased with them both, traded wealth in"
"6895","7326 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri who said: Salim informed me, that Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, rode one day with Abdillah ibn Buhaynah - and he is a man of Azd Shanuah, ally of Banu al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf, and of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - to a land of his in Rim. So Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, bought it from him upon looking at it, and Rim is from Madinah about thirty miles. So here Abdillah ibn Umar and Abdillah ibn Buhaynah, may Allah be pleased with them, traded what was absent from them, and saw that as permissible. If one says: That was only permissible due to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, stipulating option. It is said to him: That option did not become due to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from stipulation; had it become due from stipulation, the sale would have been void. Do you not see that had a man bought from a man a slave or land upon his having option therein to no known time, the sale is void. And Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in this hadith we narrated from him, did not stipulate the option of seeing to a known time. So that indicated that the option he stipulated is an option due to him by right of contract, namely the option of seeing which Talhah and Jubayr held, as we narrated from them, not a conditional option."
"6896","7327 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim who said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: When we traded, each of us had option while the two traders had not separated. He said: So I traded with Uthman - I sold him wealth of mine in the valley for what was his in Khaybar. When I traded with him, I began stepping back upon my heels backward, fearing Uthman would return the sale to me before I left him. So here Uthman ibn Affan and Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, traded what was absent from them, and saw that as permissible - and that in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and no disapprover disapproved it against them."
"6897","7328 - Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from Muhammad ibn Umayr who said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited two sales: that a man say to a man: Cast to me your garment, and I cast to you my garment, without their turning over or mutually agreeing; and say: My riding-beast for your riding-beast, without their turning over or mutually agreeing. So in this hadith is the permitting of sale by mutual agreement, and proof that the prohibited munabadhah is what Abu Hanifah, may Allah be pleased with him, held, not what his opponent held. And praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"6898","Chapter 7: The father marrying his virgin daughter - does he need to consult her in that? 7329 - Abu Zurah Abd al-Rahman ibn Umar al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The orphan girl is consulted in herself; if she is silent, she has permitted, and if she denies, she is not compelled."
"6899","7330 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The orphan girl is consulted; if she is pleased, her pleasure is hers, and if she denies, there is no binding upon her."
"6900","7331 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr who said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that a man may marry his adult virgin daughter without her command or seeking her permission, to whom he sees, and she has no view in that along with him in their view. They said: Since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aimed in the two reports mentioned at the first of this chapter, with what was mentioned therein of silence judged with the ruling of permission, at the orphan girl - she who has no father - that indicated that one with a father is therein contrary to her, and that her father's authority over her is stronger than the authority of her other guardians after her father. And of those who went to this saying is Malik ibn Anas, may Allah have mercy upon him. Others differed with them in that, and said: The guardian of the virgin, father or other, may not marry her except after consulting her therein and after her silence at his consulting her. And they said: There is not in the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) aiming in the two narrated reports therein at the first of this chapter at the orphan girl what indicates that other than the orphan girl is therein contrary to the ruling of the orphan girl. For it may be that he intended thereby all virgins, orphans and others. And he specified the orphan girl by mention, since there is no difference between her therein and other than her, and because the hearer of that from him concerning the virgin orphan infers thereby the ruling of the non-orphan virgin. And we have seen the like of this in the Quran: Allah Almighty said, concerning what is prohibited of women: Your stepdaughters in your care of your wives with whom you consummated. So He mentioned the stepdaughter in the husband's care, yet that was not upon prohibiting the stepdaughter in the husband's care to the exclusion of the stepdaughter older than him. Rather prohibition was upon them both. Likewise what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the orphan virgin is not upon the orphan virgin specifically; rather it is upon the orphan and non-orphan virgin. And what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard of that concerning the virgin orphan was proof for them that one with a father is therein likewise, since they knew that the virgin before her maturity - to her father belong contracts of sales over her wealth, and contract of marriage over her person. And they saw her maturity lift her father's guardianship over her in contracts over her wealth, so likewise it lifts from her contracts over her person. Along with this, the people of this doctrine narrated for their doctrine reports they argued with for it - except that in some is criticism upon the doctrine of the people of reports, and most are free of that. We shall bring them all, with their defects and the corruption of what the people of reports corrupt thereof in this chapter, Allah willing. Of what was narrated therein of what the people of reports criticized is what."
"6901","7332 - Abu Umayyah and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man married his daughter while she was a virgin, and she was unwilling, and she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he gave her choice. So those who go to reports, distinguishing between their narrators, affirming what the memorizers narrated of them and dropping what is below them, objected, saying: Thus Jarir ibn Hazim narrated this hadith - and he is a man of much error. The memorizers narrated it from Ayyub upon other than that - among them Sufyan al-Thawri, Hammad ibn Zayd and Ismail ibn Ulayyah. They mentioned in that what."
"6902","7333 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Ikrimah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separated between a man and a woman, whose father married her while she was unwilling, and she was previously married. So there is established thereby, in their view, the error of Jarir in this hadith in two ways: one, his inserting Ibn Abbas therein; the other, his mentioning therein that she was a virgin, while she was previously married. And of what was narrated in that also is what."
"6903","7334 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran, Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Abu Salih al-Hakam ibn Abi Musa informed us, he said: Shuayb ibn Ishaq al-Dimashqi narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man married his daughter while she was a virgin without her command, and she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he separated them. So part of the argument of one who goes in that to tracing chains is that this hadith is not known that anyone of those who narrated it from Shuayb mentioned Jabir therein other than this Abu Salih. Of those who narrated it dropping Jabir therefrom is Ali ibn Mabad."
"6904","7335 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Shuayb ibn Ishaq, from al-Awzai, from Ata, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, and he did not mention Jabir. Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated it from al-Awzai, and clarified of its corruption what is greater than this."
"6905","7336 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Salamah informed us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Murrah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that. So this hadith became from al-Awzai from Ibrahim ibn Murrah from Ata; and this Ibrahim ibn Murrah is weak in hadith, with the people of reports of knowledge no basis at all. And of what they narrated in that also, of what no one has criticism therein, is what."
"6906","7337 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him. (H). 7338 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, they said: al-Qanabi, Abdillah ibn Maslamah, informed us. (H). 7339 - And Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi Ismail ibn Maslamah narrated to us, both saying: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn al-Fadl, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim narrating, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The previously-married woman has more right to herself than her guardian, and the virgin is consulted in herself, and her permission is her silence."
"6907","7340 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Mawhab, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6908","7341 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Mawhab, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6909","7342 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ziyad ibn Sad, from Abdillah ibn al-Fadl, he heard Nafi ibn Jubayr narrating, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The previously-married woman has more right to herself than her guardian, and the virgin is consulted. Since the previously-married woman mentioned in this hadith is she whose guardian is any guardian, father or other, likewise the virgin mentioned therein is the virgin whose guardian is any guardian, father or other. That is: there was no limit therein, and its analogy is to be a limit - likewise the virgin joined to her. And this hadith has been narrated from Salih ibn Kaysan from Nafi ibn Jubayr with a wording other than this wording."
"6910","7343 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The father has no command with the previously-married woman, and the virgin is sought permission, and her permission is her silence. So its meaning is the meaning of the first, alike. And the virgin mentioned in this hadith is the virgin with a father, as the previously-married woman mentioned therein is likewise. This is what was narrated to us in this chapter from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, there was narrated in that from her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what."
"6911","7344 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah saying: Dhakwan, freedman of Aishah, said: I heard Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saying: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the young girl whom her family marries: is she consulted or not? He said: Yes, she is consulted. I said: She is shy and is silent. He said: That is her permission when she is silent. So here the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) equalized all the family of the virgin in marrying her, and did not separate therein between the ruling of her father and the ruling of other than him of the rest of her family. As for Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, there was narrated in that from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what."
"6912","7345 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The previously-married woman is not married until she is consulted, nor the virgin until she is sought permission. They said: And how is her permission, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Silence."
"6913","7346 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Wahhab informed us, from Waki, from Ali ibn al-Mubarak, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6914","7347 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us. (H). 7348 - And Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj and Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So he joined therein all guardians, and did not make for the father therein a ruling additional over the ruling of others of them. So that indicated that the meaning we mentioned in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah we narrated from Muhammad ibn Amr at the first of this chapter is as we mentioned, that its meaning agree with the meaning of this hadith, not contradict it. And if this matter is taken by way of some narrators' excellence over others in memorization, precision and eminence, then Yahya ibn Abi Kathir is more eminent than Muhammad ibn Amr, more precise and sounder in narration - Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani preferred him over the people of an era he mentioned therein."
"6915","7348A - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: I heard Ayyub saying: There does not remain upon the face of the earth the like of Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, may Allah have mercy upon him. And Muhammad ibn Amr is not in this rank, nor near it; rather a group spoke concerning him, among them Malik ibn Anas, may Allah have mercy upon him. So he narrated from him what 7348A - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman al-Badrawi narrated to us, he said: I was with Malik ibn Anas, and Muhammad ibn Amr was mentioned with him, and he said: He carried - meaning hadith - and it was carried. As for Adi al-Kindi, there was narrated from him in that, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what."
"6916","7349 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn, from Adi ibn Adi al-Kindi, from his father Adi, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The previously-married woman expresses from herself, and the virgin's pleasure is her silence."
"6917","7350 - Bahr narrated to us, from Shuayb, from al-Layth, with his isnad the like of it."
"6918","7351 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Rabi ibn Tariq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Adi ibn Adi, from his father, from al-Urs - and he is Ibn Umayrah, and he was of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the like of it. So this is like what Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this is the correction of the reports in this chapter, and it indicated that the father of the virgin does not marry her after her maturity except as her other guardians after him marry her. We already mentioned of rational examination in that at the first of this chapter what suffices us from repeating it here. With all that we take. We hold that the father of the virgin not marry his adult virgin daughter except after consulting her therein and at her silence at that consultation - and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all. And a group argued in that with what was narrated concerning the daughter of Nuaym ibn al-Nahham, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6919","7352 - Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ibrahim ibn Nuaym ibn Abdillah ibn al-Nahham, he informed him, that his father informed him, from Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: Propose for me the daughter of Abdillah ibn al-Nahham. He said to him: He has a brother's son, and he would not marry you leaving them. So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, went to Zayd ibn al-Khattab and spoke to him, and he proposed on his behalf. Ibn al-Nahham said: I would not soil my flesh and blood and raise your flesh, so he married her to his brother's son - and the inclination of the girl and her mother was to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. So the woman went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him that her father married her without consulting her, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted her marriage. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Consult women in themselves. The girl was a virgin. Ibn al-Nahham said: O Messenger of Allah, they dislike it only because he has no wealth; he has in my wealth the like of what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, gave them. They said: So in this hadith is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted upon her her father's marriage while she disliked it, since she was a virgin, and did not make for her along with her father a view in contracting marriage upon her. It was said to him: Had this hadith been sound, established as we narrated - and how can that be so when al-Layth ibn Sad narrated it, opposing Abdillah ibn Lahiah in its chain and its text."
"6920","7353 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ibrahim ibn Salih ibn Abdillah - whose name known thereby is Nuaym ibn al-Nahham, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named him Salih - that he informed him, that Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: Propose for me the daughter of Salih. He said to him: He has orphans, and he would not prefer us over them. Abdillah went to his uncle Zayd ibn al-Khattab to propose on his behalf, and Zayd ibn al-Khattab went to Salih and said: Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, sent me to you proposing your daughter. He said: I have orphans, and I would not soil my flesh and raise your flesh; I call you to witness that I have married her to so-and-so. Her mother's inclination was in Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, so she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Prophet of Allah, Abdillah ibn Umar proposed my daughter, and her father married her to an orphan in his care, without consulting her. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Salih and said: Did you marry your daughter without consulting her? He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Consult women in themselves - and she is a virgin. Salih said: I only did this because of what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, dowered her; she has in my wealth the like of what he gave her. So in this hadith is contrary to what is in the first hadith, of chain and text together: this hadith is only stopped upon Ibrahim ibn Salih, while the first, Ibrahim ibn Salih carried it to his father and to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. Upon this opponent's doctrine, what al-Layth ibn Sad narrated therein should be worthier than what Abdillah ibn Lahiah narrated, due to al-Layth's firmness and precision, and the little confusion of his hadith, and what is in Abdillah ibn Lahiah's hadith of the opposite of that. As for what is in the text of this hadith opposing Abdillah ibn Lahiah's hadith: therein is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Nuaym, when what he contracted upon his daughter of marriage without her pleasure reached him: Consult women in themselves - so that was thereby a rejection against Nuaym, because Nuaym did not consult his daughter in herself. This is the difference of what is in Abdillah ibn Lahiah's hadith. If one says: There is not in this hadith that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) annulled the marriage. It was said to him: That, in our view - and Allah knows best - is because Nuaym's daughter did not come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking him that. Only her mother came to him, not by her commissioning her with that, so that speech for her would be due for her with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So there was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what was of speech to Nuaym by way of teaching. And he did not annul the marriage, since that is by way of judgment, and judgment is only due for one present, by agreement of all Muslims. And al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man married his daughter while she was a virgin, and she was unwilling, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rejected his marriage from her. So how is it permissible to make the hadith of Nuaym ibn al-Nahham upon what Abdillah ibn Lahiah narrated, when it was returned to Abdillah ibn Umar - and this occurred, and there has been narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the contrary of that. Then we found a hadith narrated concerning the daughter of Nuaym ibn al-Nahham, indicating that she was previously married."
"6921","7354 - al-Qasim ibn Abdillah ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Musab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, from Yahya ibn Urwah, from his father, that Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: I have proposed the daughter of Nuaym ibn al-Nahham, and I want you to walk with me and speak to him for me. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I know Nuaym better than you; he has a brother's orphan son with him, and he would not settle people's flesh and soil his own. He said: Her mother has proposed to me. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If you will do it, go with your uncle Zayd ibn al-Khattab. He said: So they went to him and spoke to him. He said: It is as if he heard the saying of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Welcome to you; and he mentioned of his station and honor. Then he said: I have a brother's orphan son with me, and I would not break people's flesh and soil my own. Her mother said from the side of the house: By Allah, this shall not be until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judges it upon us; do you withhold a widow of Banu Adi for your brother's foolish son? She said: Or weak. He said: Then she went out until she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him the news. So he called Nuaym and related upon him as he said to Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Nuaym: Join your kin, and please your widow and her mother; they have of her affair a share. So in this hadith is that the daughter of Nuaym ibn al-Nahham was previously married, and that is farther from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) having permitted her father's marriage upon her while she was unwilling. And with Allah is success."
"6922","Chapter 8: The amount that prohibits charity upon its owner. 7355 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, who said: Rabiah ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Abu Kabshah al-Saluli, who said: Sahl ibn al-Hanzaliyyah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever asks people out of sufficiency only increases of the embers of Hell. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is sufficiency? He said: That he knows that with his family is what lunches and suppers them."
"6923","7356 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of it with his isnad. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that whoever owns this amount, charity is prohibited upon him, and asking is not permissible for him, and they argued for that with this hadith. Others differed with them in that, and said: Whoever owns an uqiyyah of silver - forty dirhams - or its equivalent of gold, charity is prohibited upon him, and asking is not permissible for him; whoever owns less than that, charity is not prohibited upon him. And they argued for that with what:"
"6924","7357 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from a man of Banu Asad who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him saying to a man asking: Whoever of you asks while he has an uqiyyah or its equivalent has asked importunately. And the uqiyyah then was forty dirhams. And with what:"
"6925","7358 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6926","7359 - And with what Yazid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Others differed with them in that, and said: Whoever owns fifty dirhams or its equivalent of gold, charity is prohibited upon him, and asking is not permissible for them; whoever owns less than that, charity is not prohibited upon him. And they argued for that with what:"
"6927","7360 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. (H). 7361 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, both saying: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from his father, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant asks a asking while he has what suffices him, except it comes as disgrace, or bruises, or scratches in his face on the Day of Resurrection. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is his sufficiency? He said: Fifty dirhams or its reckoning of gold."
"6928","7362 - Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Hisham al-Rifai narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he said: bruises in his face, without doubt, and added: It was said to Sufyan: Even had it been from other than Hakim? He said: Zubayd narrated it to us from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid. Others differed with them in that, and said: Whoever owns two hundred dirhams, charity and asking are prohibited upon him; whoever owns less than it, asking is not prohibited upon him, nor is charity prohibited upon him either. And they argued for that with what:"
"6929","7363 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from a man of Muzaynah, that he came to his mother and she said: O my son, had you gone to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him. He said: So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he stood preaching the people, saying: Whoever is independent, Allah enriches him; whoever is chaste, Allah makes him chaste; and whoever asks people while he has the equivalent of five awaq asks importunately. Abu Jafar said: When they differed in that, uncovering what they differed in became due, to extract from these sayings a sound saying. We saw charity is not free of one of two ways: either it be prohibited, not permissible, of the prohibited things at necessities to them; or it be permissible for him to own an amount of wealth, and it becomes prohibited upon its owner. We saw whoever owned less than what lunches him, or less than what suppers him, charity was permissible for him, by agreement of all parties. Thereby its ruling went out of the ruling of prohibited things that become permissible at necessity. Do you not see that whoever is compelled to carrion, what is permissible thereof to him is what holds his soul, not what lunches, until he have lunch, or until he have supper. Since what is permissible of charity is contrary to what is permissible of carrion at necessity, it is established that it is only prohibited upon one who owns some amount. We wanted to look into that amount, what is it? We saw whoever owned less than what lunches, or less than what suppers, was not thereby rich. Likewise whoever owned forty dirhams, or fifty dirhams, or what is less than two hundred dirhams; when he owned two hundred dirhams, he was thereby rich - because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning zakah: Take it from their rich, and place it among their poor. So we knew thereby that the owner of two hundred is rich, and what is less than it is not rich. So there is established thereby that charity is prohibited upon the owner of two hundred dirhams and above, and it is permissible for one who owns what is less than that - and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them all."
"6930","Chapter 9: Obligation of zakah in pasturing camels in what exceeds one hundred twenty. 7364 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Habib ibn Abi Habib informed us, he said: Amr ibn Harim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to me, he said: When Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz was made caliph, he sent to Madinah seeking the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Amr ibn Hazm concerning charities, and the letter of Umar. He found with the family of Amr ibn Hazm the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Amr ibn Hazm concerning charities. And he found with the family of Umar the letter of Umar concerning charities, like the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they were copied. Amr narrated to me that the family of Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman asked him to copy for them what is in those two letters, and he copy for him what is in this letter. Of what was in that letter: camels, when they exceed ninety by one, therein are two hiqqahs that the stallion mounts, until reaching one hundred twenty. When camels reach one hundred twenty, there is nothing in what exceeds thereof short of ten. When they reach one hundred thirty, therein are two daughters of labun and a hiqqah, until reaching one hundred forty. When they are one hundred forty, therein are two hiqqahs and a daughter of labun, until reaching one hundred fifty. When they are one hundred fifty, therein are three hiqqahs, then he ran the obligation likewise until reaching three hundred. When they reach three hundred, therein of every fifty a hiqqah, and of every forty a daughter of labun. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to this hadith and said with it. Others differed with them in that, and said: What exceeds one hundred twenty, in every fifty a hiqqah, and in every forty a daughter of labun. The explanation of that: had camels exceed one hundred twenty by one camel, the increase of this camel necessitates a second ruling, other than the ruling of one hundred twenty. So in every forty is a daughter of labun, then they run that likewise until the increase reaches the completion of one hundred thirty, and they place therein a hiqqah and two daughters of labun. Then it is likewise until the increase ends at one hundred forty; when they are one hundred forty, therein are two hiqqahs and a daughter of labun, to one hundred fifty. When they are one hundred fifty, therein are three hiqqahs, then they run the obligation in the increase over that likewise ever. And they argued for that of the reports with what:"
"6931","7365 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Thumamah ibn Abdillah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, when made caliph, directed Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, to al-Bahrayn, and wrote for him this letter: This is the obligation of charity which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated upon the Muslims, which Allah Almighty commanded His Messenger with; whoever of the believers is asked it upon its face, let him give it, and whoever is asked above it, let him not give it. In that letter of his was: camels, when they exceed one hundred twenty, in every forty a daughter of labun, and in every fifty a hiqqah."
"6932","7366 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Thabit al-Bunani sent me to Thumamah ibn Abdillah ibn Anas al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, to send to him the letter of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, which he wrote for Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, when he sent him as collector. Hammad said: So he handed it to me, and upon it was the seal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and therein was mention of the obligations of charities. Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Marzuq."
"6933","7367 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Musa Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Dawud, who said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the people of Yemen a letter, therein the obligations, practices and blood-moneys, and sent it with Amr ibn Hazm. Then he mentioned, in what exceeds one hundred twenty of camels, likewise also."
"6934","7368 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abdillah ibn Lahiah informed me, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah al-Ansari, from Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr al-Ansari, he informed him that this is the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Amr ibn Hazm concerning charities. He mentioned, in what exceeds one hundred twenty, likewise also."
"6935","7369 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to me, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for Amr ibn Hazm the obligations of camels. Then he mentioned, in what exceeds one hundred twenty, likewise also."
"6936","7370 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, who said: The copy of the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) written concerning charity - it is with the family of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him; Salim and Abdillah, sons of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, read it to me, and I retained it upon its face. It is what Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, may Allah have mercy upon him, copied from Salim and Abdillah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, when he was made governor over Madinah and commanded his workers to act with it. Then he mentioned this hadith. They said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, acted with that. And they mentioned in that what:"
"6937","7371 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to take upon this letter. He mentioned the obligations of camels. Of what was mentioned thereof: what exceeds one hundred twenty, in every forty a daughter of labun, and in every fifty a hiqqah. Others differed with them in that and said: What exceeds one hundred twenty of camels, the obligation is renewed therein. In every five thereof a sheep, until the increase ends at twenty-five, and therein is a daughter of makhad to one hundred forty-nine. When they are one hundred fifty, therein are three hiqqahs, then likewise the increase - what is less than fifty, therein are renewed obligations upon the ruling of the first obligations of camels; when fifty is completed, therein a hiqqah. And they argued for that of the reports with what:"
"6938","7372 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: I said to Qays ibn Sad: Write for me the letter of Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm. So he wrote it for me in a sheet, then came with it, and informed me that he took it from the letter of Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, and informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote it for his grandfather Amr ibn Hazm, may Allah be pleased with him, in mention of what comes out of the obligations of camels. Therein was: when they reach ninety, therein are two hiqqahs, until reaching one hundred twenty. When they are more than that, in every fifty a hiqqah; what remains is returned to the first obligation of camels - what is less than twenty-five, therein sheep, in every five camels a sheep."
"6939","7373 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: When they differed in that, examination became due, to extract from these three sayings a sound saying. We examined that, and saw them all make one hundred twenty the end of what became due in what exceeds ninety. I have seen what was made an end before that: when camels exceed it by something, there becomes due by its increase an obligation other than the first obligation. Of that: we found them place in five camels a sheep, then clarify to us that the ruling is likewise in what exceeds five to nine. When one is added, they obligate thereby a future ruling and place therein two sheep. Then they clarify to us that the ruling is likewise in what exceeds to fourteen; when one is added, they obligate thereby a future ruling and place therein three sheep. Then they clarify to us that the ruling is likewise in what exceeds to twenty; when they are twenty, therein are four sheep. Then they ran the obligation likewise in what exceeds to one hundred twenty: whenever they obligated something, they clarified that it is the obligatory in what they obligated it, to a known end. So whatever exceeds that end by something, the first obligation is thereby overturned to other than it, or to an addition upon it. Since that was so, and one hundred twenty was made an end for what they obligated in the increase over ninety, it is established that what exceeds twenty is due thereby something: either an addition upon the first obligation, or other than that. So there is established, by what we mentioned, the corruption of the saying of the people of the first saying, and the change of ruling by increase over one hundred twenty is established. Then we looked between the people of the second saying and the third saying, and found those who go to the second saying obligate, by the increase of one camel over one hundred twenty, returning the ruling of all camels to what becomes due therein of daughters of labun in their saying - namely what we mentioned from them that in every forty is a daughter of labun. Part of the argument against them, for the people of the third saying: we saw all that exceeds the named ends in the obligations of camels, below one hundred twenty, the ruling changes by that increase, and that increase has a share in what becomes due thereby. Of that: in twenty-four are four sheep; when one is added, therein is a daughter of makhad to thirty-five. When one is added, therein is a daughter of labun - so the daughter of makhad was obligatory in twenty-five, not in part of it. Likewise the daughter of labun is obligatory in all thirty-six, not in part of it; likewise the rest of obligations in camels, until ending at one hundred twenty - the obligation does not move by an increase in which is nothing; rather it moves by an increase in which is something. Do you not see that in ten camels are two sheep; when a camel is added, there is nothing therein, and its increase does not change the ruling of the ten before it. When camels are fifteen, therein are three sheep - so the obligation was obligatory in the camel with which what necessitates three sheep is completed, and in what is before it. Since what we mentioned was so, and camels, when they exceed one hundred twenty camels by one camel - all agreed there is nothing in this camel, because those who obligated renewing the obligation did not obligate therein anything, nor change thereby a ruling. And those who did not obligate renewing the obligation, of the people of the second saying, placed in every forty of one hundred twenty a daughter of labun, and did not place in the camel exceeding that anything. Since it is established that the obligation before one hundred twenty does not move except by what becomes due therein of part of the obligation due thereby, and the camel exceeding one hundred twenty has nothing due therein of an obligation due thereby, it is established that it does not change the obligation of other than it from what it was before its occurrence. So there is established, by what we mentioned, the saying of one who went to the third saying - and of those who went to it are Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah's mercy be upon them. And that has also been narrated from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him."
"6940","7374 - Ismail ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Abu Ubaydah and Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam, from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said concerning the obligations of camels: When they exceed ninety, therein are two hiqqahs to one hundred twenty. When they reach one hundred twenty, the obligation is renewed with sheep; in every five a sheep. When they reach twenty-five, then the obligations of camels. When camels are many, in every fifty a hiqqah. And that has also been narrated from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, may Allah have mercy upon him."
"6941","7375 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, who said: Ibrahim al-Nakhai said: When camels exceed one hundred twenty, they are returned to the first obligation. If the people of the second saying argue for their doctrine, saying: the meaning of the connected reports testifies to our saying, and that is not with our opponent. It is said to them: Upon your doctrine, most of them do not establish proof for you against your opponent; had he argued against you with the like thereof, you would not have allowed him, and you would have made him, by his arguing thereby against you, ignorant of hadith. Of that: the hadith of Thumamah ibn Abdillah was only connected by Abdillah ibn al-Muthanna alone; we know none who connected it other than him. And you do not make Abdillah ibn al-Muthanna a proof. Then came Hammad ibn Salamah - whose standing with the people of knowledge in knowledge is more eminent than Abdillah ibn al-Muthanna's standing, and he is of those adduced as proof - and narrated this hadith from Thumamah disconnected. So upon your principles, this hadith must enter the meaning of the disconnected, and exit the meaning of the connected, because you hold that the addition of a non-memorizer over the memorizer is not heeded. As for the hadith of al-Zuhri from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, only Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated it from al-Zuhri. I heard Ibn Abi Dawud saying: This Sulayman ibn Dawud and Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Harrani are both weak in their view. And Sulayman ibn Dawud who narrates from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz is firm in their view. Of what also indicates the feebleness of this hadith: the companions of al-Zuhri from whom his knowledge is taken - like Yunus ibn Yazid, and whoever narrated from al-Zuhri therein anything - only narrated from him the sheet with the family of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. Do you see al-Zuhri having the obligations of camels, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from his grandfather - all imams and people of knowledge taken from - then be silent of that, and be compelled to return to Umar's sheet, unnarrated, and narrate the people with it? This, in our view, is of what is not permissible upon his like. If one says: The hadith of Mamar from Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr is a connected hadith, with no criticism for anyone therein. It is said to him: It is not connected; Mamar only narrated it from Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather - and his grandfather is Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, who did not see the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor was born except after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote this letter to his father, because he was only born in Najran, before the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) death in the tenth year of Hijrah. It was not conveyed to us in this hadith that Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm narrated this hadith from his father. So the disconnection of this hadith is also established - and the disconnected, you do not argue with it. So it is established that all narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter is disconnected. If you do not allow your opponent arguing with the disconnected in other than this chapter, why do you argue against him in this chapter? If lack of connection in one place must remove acceptance of report, it must be likewise in all places. And if the report must be accepted, though its chain is unconnected, due to the trust of who carried it to him in one chapter, it must be accepted in all chapters. If one says: As for the hadith of Amr ibn Hazm, it is confused and differed in, so no proof therein for any of the people of these sayings, and other than it of what was narrated in this chapter is worthier. It is said to him: Whence is the hadith of Amr ibn Hazm confused? As for Qays ibn Sad, he narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, as we mentioned from him - and Qays is a proof, memorizer. As for the hadith of al-Zuhri opposing it, only one whose narration from al-Zuhri you do not accept narrated it from al-Zuhri, due to his weakness in your view. As for the hadith of Mamar, he only narrated it from Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father - and Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr is not in firmness and precision like Qays ibn Sad. And indeed:"
"6942","7376 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to me, he said: I heard Ibn al-Wazir saying: I heard al-Shafii saying: I heard Sufyan ibn Uyaynah saying: When we saw a man writing hadith from one of four - he mentioned among them Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr - we mocked him, because they did not know hadith. Since Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr did not match Qays in precision and memorization, the hadith became with us upon what Qays narrated - especially since Qays mentioned that Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad wrote it for him. And Allah knows best."
"6943","Book of Bequests, Chapter 1: What bequests are permissible in of wealth, and what the sick does in his illness in which he dies, of gifts, charities and manumissions. 7377 - Ali ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from his father who said: I fell ill in the year of the Conquest an illness from which I neared death. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me visiting me, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have much wealth, and none inherits me except my daughter; shall I give all my wealth in charity? He said: No. I said: Shall I give two-thirds of my wealth in charity? He said: No. He said: Then half? He said: No. I said: Then a third? He said: A third, and a third is much."
"6944","7378 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Musab ibn Sad, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited me, and I said: Shall I bequeath all my wealth? He said: No. I said: Then half? He said: No. I said: Then a third? He said: Yes, and a third is much."
"6945","7379 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman who said: Sad said, then he mentioned the like thereof. Abu Jafar said: So people spoke concerning a man: may he bequeath a third of his wealth, or should he fall short of that? A group said: He may bequeath a full third of his wealth, in what he loves, in what bequests are permissible. And they argued for that with the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting Sad to bequeath a third of his wealth, after preventing him bequeathing more than that, as we mentioned in these reports. And with what:"
"6946","7380 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn Amr al-Hadrami informed me, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty made for you a third of your wealth at the end of your lives, as increase in your deeds. Others differed with them in that, and said: The bequeather should fall short in his bequest of a third of his wealth, due to the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A third, and a third is much. Of what was narrated in that from the early ones who went to it is what:"
"6947","7381 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah who said: Ibn Abbas used to say: Fall short of the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): It is much."
"6948","7382 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Humayd informed me, from Bakr ibn Abdillah who said: I bequeathed to Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari. He said: I would not accept the bequest of a man with a child, bequeathing a third. Part of the argument for the people of the first saying against the people of this saying is that had bequeathing a third been injustice, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have disapproved that against Sad, and said to him: Fall short of a third. Since he left that, he had permitted it to him. Therein is the establishment of what the people of the first saying held - and of those who went to that are Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy upon them."
"6949","7383 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Zadham narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, that a man freed six slaves of his at death, with no wealth other than them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast lots among them, and freed two, and enslaved four."
"6950","7384 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Imran, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6951","7385 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ata al-Khurasani narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib; and Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Imran ibn Husayn. And Qatadah, Humayd, and Simak ibn Harb, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn - and he mentioned the like of it."
"6952","7386 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad and Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So here the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made manumission in illness from the third - likewise gifts and charities. One of those who went to this saying also argued with the hadith of al-Zuhri, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited him in his illness, and he said: Shall I give all my wealth in charity? He said: No, until he returned him to a third, as we mentioned at the first of this chapter. He said: So in this hadith is that he made his charity in his illness from the third, like his bequests of a third after his death. Against him enters for his opponent that Musab ibn Sad narrated this hadith from his father: his asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein was only upon bequest with charity after death, as we mentioned from him at the first of this chapter. So what he argued with of Amir's hadith is not worthier than what his opponent argued against him with of Musab's hadith. Then people spoke after this concerning one who frees six slaves of his at his death, with no wealth other than them, and the heirs refuse to permit. A group said: A third of them are freed, and they work for what remains of their value - and of those who said that are Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy upon them. Others said: A third of them are freed, and what remains of them is slaves for the freedman's heirs. Others said: Lots are cast among them, and whoever is drawn of the third is freed, and the rest remain enslaved. And they argued for that with what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Imran. Part of the argument for the people of the first two sayings against the people of this saying was that what they mentioned of the lots mentioned in Imran's hadith is abrogated; lots were in the beginning of Islam used in things, and judged with therein, and what was drawn thereof - the thing for which the lot was - made specific. Of that: what Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, judged with in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Yemen, what:"
"6953","7387 - Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Awn - or Yala ibn Ubayd, I doubt - narrated to us, from al-Ajlah ibn Abdillah, from al-Shabi, from Abdillah ibn al-Khalil al-Hadrami, from Zayd ibn Arqam, who said: While I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man of Yemen came to him, with Ali then there. He said: O Messenger of Allah, three men came to Ali disputing over a child, having lain with a woman in one purity, and he cast lots among them, and one of them was drawn, and the child was handed to him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until his molars appeared - or said: his teeth. So here the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not disapprove against Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, what he judged with of lots in the party's claim of the child. So that indicated that the ruling then was likewise, then it was abrogated after, by our agreement and this opponent's agreement. What we narrated in the chapter of physiognomy indicates its abrogation: Ali's ruling in the like thereof making the child between both claimants jointly, inheriting them and inherited by them. So that indicated that the ruling then was Ali's ruling with what he judged in everything like the lineage the party claims, and wealth bequeathed by the party - after he bequeathed it to each singly - or manumission freed by slaves in their freedman's illness: lots be cast among them, and whichever is drawn deserves what he claimed, and what became due by bequest and manumission. Then that was abrogated by the abrogation of usury, when things were returned to known measures wherein is equity, with no increase nor decrease. After this: what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged, of manumission in illness, of lots, and making it of the third, is not free of one of two ways. Either it be a ruling proving the rest of the sick's acts in his illness, of his manumissions, gifts and charities. Or it be a ruling in the sick's manumission specifically, to the exclusion of his other acts, gifts and charities. If it is specific in manumission, to the exclusion of other than it, then what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made in this hadith, of manumission in the third, should not be proof upon gifts and charities that they are likewise. So the saying of one who says: they are of all wealth, is established - since rational examination testified for him, though this is not grasped therein opposing what he said except by imitation, and there is nothing in this chapter conveyed other than this hadith. And if the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made that manumission in the third proof for us that the sick's gifts and charities are likewise, then likewise it is proof for us that lots were therein all current and judged with. So in its lifting, with us and with this opponent of ours, from gifts and charities, is proof that its lifting is also from manumission. Thereby the saying of one who went to lots falls, and one of the other two sayings is established. One who went to affirming lots said: How can lots be abrogated, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) acted with them in what Muslims agreed upon acting with therein after him? They mentioned what:"
"6954","7388 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah; and Said ibn al-Musayyib, Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah, and Alqamah ibn Waqqas, from Aishah who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended travel, he cast lots among his wives; whichever one's lot came out, he went out with her."
"6955","7389 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6956","7390 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq who said: Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah. And from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah, and from Alqamah ibn Waqqas; and Said ibn al-Musayyib and Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from Aishah. And Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from Aishah - the like of it."
"6957","7391 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Isa ibn Talid narrated to us, he said: al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah al-Qitbani narrated to us, from Abu al-Tahir Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his uncle Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm who said: My maternal aunt Amrah bint Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from Aishah, the like of it. They said: This is what people should do to today, and it is not abrogated - so why do they deny that lots in manumission in illness is likewise. It was said to them: We mentioned therein in its place what suffices; but we mention here also what therein is proof that you have no argument in this, Allah willing. Muslims agreed that a man may travel whither he loves, though that travel be long, with none of his wives with him, and the ruling of division is lifted from him by his travel. Since that was so, the lots of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among his wives was at the time of his need to go out with one of them, to please her of them with whom he does not go out, and to be known that he did not favor her with whom he went out over them; since he might go out leaving them all, he might go out leaving whom he wills of them. So there is established, by what we mentioned, that lots are only used in what may be left, and in what he may execute without them. Of that: two litigants present before the judge, each claiming against his companion a claim. The judge should cast lots between them; whichever is drawn, he begins examining his affair - and he may examine the affair of whom he wills of them without lots. The better for him, to distance suspicion in this, is using lots, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used them in the affair of his wives. Likewise Muslims acted in their divisions with lots, in what they equalized among its people - with what, had they executed it among them, not by lots, would have been straight. So they cast lots among them, to settle their hearts, and lift suspicion from who undertook their division. Had he cast lots among them upon portions of goods belonging to them, before equalizing and equating its value upon their shares thereof, that division would be void. So there is established thereby that lots were only done after what permits division thereby preceded, and they were only intended to remove suspicion, not by an obligatory ruling. Likewise we say: every lot like this is good, and every lot intended to necessitate a ruling, and cut prior rights, is unused. Then we returned to the other two sayings, and saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged in the slave between two, when one of them frees him: he is all free, and he guarantees if solvent, or if insolvent. Therein of disagreement is what we mentioned in the Book of Manumission. Then we found in the hadith of Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from his father, that a man freed a share of his in a slave, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is all free, he has no partner. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified the cause for which his partner's share was freed. So that indicated that manumission, whenever it falls in part of the slave, spreads in all of him. We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged in the slave between two, when one of them frees him, with no wealth, judged upon him therein with guarantee by working upon the slave, in the share of who did not free. So there is established thereby that the ruling of these slaves in illness is likewise: since it became impossible for others to guarantee what exceeds the third of the dead, to bequeath it, and own it in his illness to whom he loves of their value, working upon them became due for the heirs. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy upon them."
"6958","7392 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas who said: When this verse came down: You shall not attain righteousness until you spend of what you love, or said: Who is he who lends Allah a good loan, Abu Talhah came and said: O Messenger of Allah, my garden in such-and-such place is for Allah; had I been able to conceal it, I would not have announced it. He said: Place it among your poor kin, or your poor family."
"6959","7393 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Thumamah who said: Anas said: Abu Talhah had land, and he made it for Allah Almighty. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to him: Place it among your poor kin. So he placed it for Hassan and Ubayy. My father said, from Thumamah, from Anas who said: They were nearer to him than I. So here Abu Talhah placed it for Ubayy and Hassan, though he and Ubayy meet at his seventh father: Abu Talhah's name is Zayd ibn Sahl ibn al-Aswad ibn Haram ibn Amr ibn Zayd Manah, son of Adi ibn Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar. And Ubayy is ibn Kab ibn Qays ibn Atik ibn Zayd ibn Muawiyah ibn Awn ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not disapprove against Abu Talhah what he did thereof. So what we mentioned indicated that whoever meets a man at his fifth father, or sixth, or above of the known fathers, is kin to him - as whoever meets him at a father below it is also kin. Allah Almighty also commanded His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to warn his nearest kin. There was narrated from him therein what."
"6960","7394 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Makhlad al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Abd al-Quddus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abbad ibn Abdillah who said: Ali said: When: Warn your nearest kin, came down, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ali, gather for me Banu Hashim - forty men, or forty less a man - then he mentioned the hadith. So in this hadith is that he aimed at the sons of his third father. And there has also been narrated from him therein what."
"6961","7395 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Makhlad, Abu al-Hasan al-Asbahani, narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Ghaffar ibn al-Qasim, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abdillah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Except that he said: Gather for me Banu Abd al-Muttalib. He said: Forty men, more or less a man. So in this hadith is that he aimed at the sons of his second father. And there has also been narrated from him therein what."
"6962","7396 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq and Zuhayr ibn Amr, who said: When: Warn your nearest kin, came down, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to a pile of a mountain, climbed its top, then said: O sons of Abd Manaf, I am a warner. So in this hadith is that he aimed at the sons of his fourth father."
"6963","7397 - And there has also been narrated from him therein what Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad and Hassan ibn Ghalib narrated to us, they said: Dimam narrated to us, from Musa ibn Wardan, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: O sons of Hashim, O sons of Qusayy, O sons of Abd Manaf, I am the warner, death the raider, and the Hour the promised. So in this hadith is that he called the sons of his fifth father. And there has also been narrated from him therein what."
"6964","7398 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and Affan narrated to us, from Abu Awanah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Abu Hurayrah who said: When: Warn your nearest kin, came down, the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and said: O sons of Kab ibn Luayy, save yourselves from the Fire; O sons of Abd Manaf, save yourselves from the Fire; O sons of Hashim, save yourselves from the Fire; O Fatimah daughter of Muhammad, save yourself from the Fire; I own for you nothing from Allah, except that you have kinship, I shall wet it with its wetting. So in this hadith is that he called them with them, the sons of his seventh father - because he is Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim ibn Abd Manaf ibn Qusayy ibn Kilab ibn Murrah ibn Kab ibn Luayy. And there has also been narrated from him therein what."
"6965","7399 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas who said: When: Warn your nearest kin, came down, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) climbed al-Safa and began calling: O sons of Fihr, O sons of Adi, O sons of so-and-so - for clans of Quraysh - until they gathered. When a man could not go out, he sent a messenger to see; Abu Lahab and Quraysh came and gathered. He said: What think you, had I informed you that horses in the valley intend to raid you, would you believe me? They said: Yes, we tried upon you only truth. He said: I am a warner to you before severe punishment. So in this hadith is that he called all the clans of Quraysh. And the like thereof has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah."
"6966","7400 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: Said and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman said: Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when: Warn your nearest kin, was revealed upon him: O company of Quraysh, buy yourselves from Allah; I avail you nothing from Allah. O sons of Abd Manaf, buy yourselves from Allah; I avail you nothing from Allah. O Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, I avail you nothing from Allah. O Safiyyah, aunt of the Messenger of Allah, I avail you nothing from Allah. O Fatimah daughter of Muhammad, I avail you nothing from Allah."
"6967","7401 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab who said: Said and Abu Salamah informed me, that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: O Safiyyah, O Fatimah. So in this hadith also is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when Allah Almighty commanded him to warn his nearest kin, called the clans of Quraysh - among them who meets him at his second father, among them who meets him at his third father, among them who meets him at his fourth father, among them who meets him at his fifth father, among them who meets him at his sixth father, and among them who meets him at his fathers above that - except that he is of those whom Quraysh joined him with. Thereby the saying of the people of this saying falls, and one of the other sayings is established. We looked into the saying of one who preferred near kin over farther kin. We found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he divided the share of near kin, generalized it with Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib - and some of Banu Hashim are nearer to him than some, and some of Banu al-Muttalib are also nearer to him than some. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not prefer thereof one near in kin to him over one farther in kin to him, and made them all kin to him, deserving what Allah Almighty made for his kinship, likewise one far in kin in a bequest to so-and-so's kin deserves not by nearness of his kin anything of what was made for his kinship except as the rest of his kin deserve, whose kin from him is farther than his kin. This is an argument. Another argument: Abu Talhah, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to place his land among the poor of kin, placed it for Hassan and Ubayy - though he and Ubayy meet at his seventh father, and he and Hassan meet at his third father, for Hassan is ibn Thabit ibn al-Mundhir ibn Haram, and Abu Talhah is Zayd ibn Sahl ibn al-Aswad ibn Haram. Abu Talhah did not prefer therein Hassan, for nearness of his kin to him, over Ubayy, for farness of his kin to him; nor did they see either deserving for his kinship thereof except as the other deserves thereof. Thereby the corruption of this saying is established. Then we returned to what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy upon him, went to, and saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he divided the share of near kin, gave all Banu Hashim - among them whose kin to him is prohibited kin, and among them whose kin to him is unprohibited. And he gave Banu al-Muttalib with them, all their kin to him unprohibited. Likewise Abu Talhah gave Ubayy and Hassan what he gave them, upon their being kin, and the lifting of prohibition from their kin to him did not expel them from his kinship. Thereby also falls what Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy upon him, went to. Then we returned to what Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy upon them both, went to, and saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the share of near kin to Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib - and he and none of them join to a father since the Hijrah; they only join, he and they, at fathers in ignorance. Likewise Abu Talhah, Ubayy and Hassan do not join at an Islamic father; they only join at a father in ignorance - and that did not prevent them being kin to him, deserving what was made for kinship. Likewise the kin of the bequeather: for his kin, nothing prevents them from that bequest except their and his not joining at a father since the Hijrah. Thereby the saying of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy upon them both, falls, and the other saying is established. So it is established that bequest thereby is: for all whose lineage stops at a father other than a father and a mother other than a mother, until he and the bequeather for his kin meet at one grandfather, in ignorance or in Islam - after those fathers be such that by kinship they deserve inheritances at times, and testimonies stand by a man of them, upon its course what is between the bequeather for his kin and them, of fathers and mothers. This saying is the sounder of the two sayings, in our view."
"6968","Book of Obligatory Shares, Chapter 1: A man dies leaving a daughter, a sister and other kin. 7402 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Asad informed me, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Attach wealth to the obligations; what the obligations leave, for the nearest male man."
"6969","7403 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6970","7404 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, and he did not mention Ibn Abbas."
"6971","7405 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6972","7406 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Mamar and Sufyan informed me, from Ibn Tawus, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that had a man died leaving his daughter, his paternal brother, and his full sister, his daughter has half, and the rest for his full brother, to the exclusion of his full sister - and they argued for that with this hadith. They also said: Had there been with the daughter no brother, but a sister and kin, the daughter has half, and the rest for the kin, however distant - and they argued for that also with what was narrated from Ibn Abbas."
"6973","7407 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, from Mamar, from Ibn Tawus who said: My father informed me, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: Allah Almighty said: If a man perishes having no child and has a sister, she has half of what he leaves. Ibn Abbas said: So you say: She has half, even if he has a child. Others differed with them in that, and said: Rather the daughter has half, and the rest between brother and sister, for the male the like of two females' share. If there is with the daughter none but the sister, the daughter has half, and the sister what remains. Part of the argument for them in that was that the hadith of Ibn Abbas they mentioned, as we mentioned at the first of this chapter, its meaning - in our view - is not upon what they carried it upon. Rather its meaning, in our view - and Allah knows best - is what the obligations leave after the shares, for the nearest male man - like a paternal aunt and paternal uncle: the rest for the uncle, to the exclusion of the aunt, because they are in one degree, equal in lineage, and the uncle is preferred over the aunt therein by being male. This is the meaning of his saying: What the obligations leave, for the nearest male man - and sister with her brother are not included in that. The proof of what we mentioned, thereof, is that they agreed in a daughter, son's daughter and son's son: the daughter has half, and the rest between son's son and son's daughter, for the male the like of two females' share - and they did not make what remains after the daughter's share for the son's son specifically, to the exclusion of the son's daughter. The meaning of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: What the obligations leave, for the nearest male man, was not upon that; it is upon other than it. Since it is established that this is outside it by their agreement, and it is established that uncle and aunt are inside it by their agreement - since they made what remains after the daughter's share for the uncle to the exclusion of the aunt. Then they differed in sister with brother: a group said: They are like aunt with uncle; others said: They are like son's son and son's daughter. So we examined that, to attach what they differed in thereof to what they agreed upon. We saw the agreed basis: son's son and son's daughter, had there been none but them, wealth would be between them, for the male the like of two females' share. When there is with them a daughter, she has half, and what remains after that half is between son's son and son's daughter, upon the like of what would be for them of all wealth, had there been with them no daughter. And uncle and aunt, had there been with them no daughter, wealth would be, by their agreement, for the uncle to the exclusion of the aunt. When there is a daughter, she has half, and what remains after that is for the uncle to the exclusion of the aunt. So what remains after the daughter's share is for who would have all wealth, had there been no daughter. Since that was so, and brother and sister, had there been with them no daughter, wealth would be between them, for the male the like of two females' share - rational examination upon that is that they be likewise, when there is with them a daughter: she must have half the wealth, due to Allah Almighty's obligation for her, and what remains after that half be between brother and sister, as would be for them all wealth, had there been no daughter - by analogy and examination, upon what we mentioned thereof. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicated what we mentioned."
"6974","7408 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun and Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us. (H). 7409 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, who said: Sulayman ibn Rabiah and Abu Musa al-Ashari were approached concerning a daughter, son's daughter and sister. They said: For the daughter half, and for the sister half. Then they said: Come to Abdillah, he will follow us. So he came to him. Abdillah said: I would have gone astray then, and I am not of the guided; but I shall judge therein with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged: for the daughter half, for the son's daughter a sixth completing two-thirds, and the rest for the sister."
"6975","7410 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl, the like of it. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made sisters from the father with the daughter residuaries - so they become with daughters in the ruling of males of brothers from the father. So the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What the obligations leave, for the nearest male man, is because he is a residuary, and no residuary nearer than him. When there is a residuary nearer than that man, wealth is for it. Upon this meaning, this hadith should be carried, so it does not oppose this hadith of Ibn Masud, nor contradict it. The way of reports is to be carried upon agreement, as a way is found to that, not upon disunity and contradiction. Had the hadith of Ibn Abbas been upon what our opponent carried it upon, it would follow upon his doctrine to contradict thereby the hadith of Ibn Masud - because this hadith of Ibn Masud is straight in chain, sound in coming. And the hadith of Ibn Abbas is confused in chain, because one not below who raised it cut it, as we mentioned at the first of this chapter. As for what they argued with of the saying of Allah Almighty: If a man perishes having no child and has a sister, she has half of what he leaves. They said: Allah Almighty only bequeathed the sister when he has no child. The argument against them therein is that Allah Almighty also said: He inherits her if she has no child. All agreed that had she left her daughter and her paternal brother, the daughter has half, and the rest for the brother. The meaning of the saying of Allah Almighty: If she has no child, is only upon a child taking all inheritance, not upon a child not taking all inheritance. Rational examination upon that, also, is that His saying Almighty: If a man perishes having no child and has a sister, she has half of what he leaves, is upon a child taking all inheritance, not upon a child not taking all inheritance. As for what they argued with of Ibn Abbas's doctrine therein, he therein opposed the rest of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides him. Of what was narrated from them therein is what."
"6976","7411 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Uqayl, that he heard Ibn Shihab informing, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that Umar ibn al-Khattab divided inheritance between daughter and sister in halves."
"6977","7412 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed me, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, divided wealth in two halves, between daughter and sister."
"6978","7413 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Israil informed me, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Ali and Abdillah, concerning a daughter and sister: for the daughter half, and for the sister half. The Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of that, except Ibn Abbas and Ibn al-Zubayr."
"6979","7414 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun and Abu Nuaym informed us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Masruq, from Abdillah, concerning a daughter, sister and grandfather, who said: Of four."
"6980","7415 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha who said: I heard al-Aswad ibn Yazid saying: Muadh judged among us in Yemen, concerning a man who left his daughter and his sister: he gave the daughter half, and gave the sister half. 7416 - Shubah said: al-Amash informed me, he said: I heard Ibrahim narrating from al-Aswad who said: Muadh judged among us in Yemen while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was alive, the like of it."
"6981","7417 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, who said: Ibn al-Zubayr judged concerning a daughter and sister: he gave the daughter half, and gave the kin the rest of the wealth. I said: Muadh judged among us in Yemen, and gave the daughter half, and gave the sister half. Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr said: You are my messenger to Abdillah ibn Utbah, and narrate to him this hadith - and he was the judge of Kufah. So here Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr returned from his saying in which he agreed with Ibn Abbas, to the saying of the others."
"6982","7418 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, they said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid who said: Muadh came to Yemen, and was asked concerning a daughter and sister: he gave the daughter half, and the sister half."
"6983","7419 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, from Mabad ibn Khalid, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, concerning two daughters, son's daughters and son's sons, and concerning two full sisters, and paternal brothers and sisters: that she shared between son's daughters, son's sons, and sons of brothers and sisters from the father, in what remains. He said: Abdillah would not share between them. And a group said, concerning a daughter and kin: the daughter has all wealth, and nothing for the kin. Sufficient ignorance in them, in their abandoning the saying of all jurists to a saying not known to have been said before them, of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor of their followers - besides that what they held thereof, its corruption is by the text of the Quran, because Allah Almighty says: Allah enjoins you concerning your children, for the male the like of two females' share. So Allah Almighty clarified to us thereby how is the ruling of children in inheritances, when male or female. Then Allah Almighty said: If they be women more than two, for them two-thirds of what he leaves. So He clarified to us the ruling of children in inheritances, when women. Then Allah Almighty said: If she be one, for her half - and clarified to us the ruling of the single daughter's inheritance. Since He clarified to us children's inheritances upon these ways, we knew thereby that the single one's inheritance ruling does not exit these three ways. It is impossible that Allah Almighty name for the daughter half, and for daughters two-thirds, and they have more than that except for another meaning He clarifies in His Book, or upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as He clarified in inheritances of kin. Had the daughter inherited all wealth, to the exclusion of kin, Allah Almighty's mentioning half would have no meaning, and He would have neglected her affair, as He neglected the son. Since He clarified for her what we mentioned, it was a delimitation from Him - Almighty - to us, upon what He named for her thereof is her share, as what He named for sisters from the father and mother by His saying: If a man is inherited kalalah or a woman, and he has a brother or sister, for each of them a sixth; if they be more than that, they are partners in a third. So what remains after what was named for them is for the residuaries. Likewise of what was named for husband and wife: what remains after what was named for them is for the residuary. Likewise the daughter also: what remains after what was named for her is for the residuary. This is an upright sound proof in this verse. Then we returned to His saying Almighty: If a man perishes having no child and has a sister - and He did not clarify to us here who that child is. So what preceded of His saying in the verse wherein we stood upon children's shares indicated to us that that child is what preceded of the child for whom He named the obligation in the other verse. Then there has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in what we mentioned, also."
"6984","7420 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays informed me, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the wife of Sad ibn al-Rabi came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Sad was killed with you, leaving his two daughters, leaving me and his brother - and his brother took his wealth, and women are only married for what is theirs. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said: Give his wife an eighth, his two daughters two-thirds, and for you the rest."
"6985","7421 - Yunus and Bahr narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6986","Chapter 2: Inheritances of kin. 7423 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Nafi narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, that a man of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, a man perished, leaving his paternal aunt and maternal aunt. He asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he stood upon his donkey, and he stopped, then raised his hands and said: O Allah, a man perished leaving his paternal aunt and maternal aunt. The man asks him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does that three times, then he said: Nothing for them."
"6987","7424 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Maysarah, Hisham ibn Sad and Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was called to a funeral of the Ansar; when he came to it, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: What did he leave? They said: He left his paternal aunt and maternal aunt. Then he advanced and said: Stop the donkey. So they stopped the donkey, and he said: O Allah, a man left his paternal aunt and maternal aunt, and nothing came down upon him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I find nothing for them."
"6988","7425 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Mutarrif informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam; and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mujir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar who said: A man of Ahl al-Aliyah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, a man perished, leaving a paternal aunt and maternal aunt; go and divide his inheritance. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) followed him upon a donkey and said: O Lord, a man left a paternal aunt and maternal aunt. Then he went a while, then said: O Lord, a man left a paternal aunt and maternal aunt. Then he went a while, then said: O Lord, a man left a paternal aunt and maternal aunt. Then he said: I do not see anything coming down upon me; nothing for them. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that when a man dies leaving kin, not a residuary, and leaving no residuary other than him, he inherits nothing of his wealth - and they argued for that with this hadith. Others differed with them in that, and said: Kin inherits, when not a residuary, by the kin between him and the dead, as is inherited by the kin he connects with - so for the paternal aunt two-thirds, and for the maternal aunt a third, because she connects with the mother's kin. Part of the argument for them in that was that this hadith with which their opponent argues against them is disconnected, and of this opponent's doctrine is not to argue with the disconnected. So how does he argue against them with what, had they argued against him with it, he would not have allowed them. Then had this hadith been established, there would not be therein either, in our view, proof in repelling inheritances of kin; it may be: Nothing for them - that is, no named obligation for them, as for others of the women who inherit, like daughters, sisters and grandmothers. Nothing came down upon him, and he said: Nothing for them, upon this meaning. It may also be: Nothing for them - no inheritance for them at all, because there had not then come down upon him: Kin are nearer to one another in Allah's Book. When it came down upon him, he made inheritance for them. It has been narrated from him in the like thereof also what."
"6989","7426 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi ibn Habban, who said: Thabit ibn al-Dahdah died, and he was an immigrant with no known origin. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Asim ibn Adi: Do you know for him among you a lineage? He said: No, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Abu Lubabah ibn Abd al-Mundhir, his nephew, and gave him his inheritance. So here the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bequeathed Abu Lubabah from Thabit, by his kin between him and him. Thereby inheritances of kin are established. The asking of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his Lord, glory be to Him, in the hadith of Ata ibn Yasar, concerning paternal and maternal aunt - whether they have inheritance or not - indicated that nothing had come down upon him before therein. Thereby the lateness of this hadith of Wasi is established, after the hadith of Ata ibn Yasar - so it abrogated it. If you say: This hadith of Wasi is disconnected. It is said to you: The hadith of Ata ibn Yasar is also disconnected - so who made you worthier of the disconnected's firmness in what agrees with you than your opponent in what agrees with him? There has been narrated the like thereof from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in reports of connected chains. Of them is what."
"6990","7427 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us. (H). 7428 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, from Hakim ibn Hakim ibn Abbad ibn Hunayf, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, that a man threw at a man an arrow and killed him, with no heir except a maternal uncle. Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah wrote therein to Umar ibn al-Khattab. Umar wrote: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah and His Messenger are the guardian of who has no guardian, and the maternal uncle is heir of who has no heir."
"6991","7429 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Tawus, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The maternal uncle is heir of who has no heir."
"6992","7430 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, and did not raise it."
"6993","7431 - Abu Yahya Ahmad ibn Zakariyya ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Maysarah al-Makki narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Abu Yahya said: I see he raised it."
"6994","7432 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, Budayl al-Uqayli said: Ali ibn Abi Talhah informed me, from Rashid ibn Sad, from Abu Amir al-Hawzani, from al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever leaves a burden, upon me. Shubah said: Perhaps he said: Then to me. Whoever leaves wealth, for his heirs. And I am heir of who has no heir: I pay his blood-money and inherit him. And the maternal uncle is heir of who has no heir: he pays for him and inherits him."
"6995","7433 - Ibn Abi Maysarah narrated to us, he said: Badal ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6996","7434 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Budayl, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he said: I inherit his wealth, and free his captive; and the maternal uncle is heir of who has no heir, and frees his captive."
"6997","7435 - Ibn Abi Maysarah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6998","7436 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Rashid ibn Sad narrated to me, that he heard al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Allah and His Messenger are the guardian of who has no guardian: He inherits his wealth, and frees his captive. And the maternal uncle is heir of who has no heir: he inherits his wealth and frees his captive. So these are connected reports, consecutively narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), agreeing with what Wasi ibn Habban narrated, and opposing what Ata ibn Yasar narrated. All that was strengthened and clarified by the saying of Allah Almighty: Kin are nearer to one another in Allah's Book. Our opponent said: You have no proof in this verse for what you held thereof, because people used to inherit by adoption - as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) adopted Zayd ibn Harithah, and it was said: Zayd ibn Muhammad; whoever did this, the adopted inherited his wealth, to the exclusion of his other kin. And people used to contract in ignorance that a man inherits a man. So Allah Almighty sent down: Kin are nearer to one another in Allah's Book, repelling that, and returning inheritances to kin. And He said: Call them by their fathers, that is more just with Allah. And they mentioned in that what."
"6999","7437 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn informed us, from Isa ibn al-Harith who said: The brother of Shurayh ibn al-Harith had a slave-girl; she bore a girl, and she grew and he married her, and she bore a boy, and the grandmother died. The brother of Shurayh and the boy disputed to Shurayh. He said: Shurayh began saying: He has no inheritance in Allah Almighty's Book; he is only a daughter's son. And he judged for the boy with inheritance, saying: Kin are nearer to one another in Allah's Book. He said: Maysarah ibn Yazid rode to Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr, and narrated to him what Shurayh judged. He said: Ibn al-Zubayr wrote to Shurayh: Maysarah narrated to me that you judged so, and said thereat: Kin are nearer to one another in Allah's Book, Almighty - those verses were only concerning residuaries in ignorance. A man in ignorance would contract a man, saying: You inherit me and I inherit you. When this verse came down, that was left. He said: The letter was presented to Shurayh and he read it, and said: Only its belly's fish freed her, and he refused to return from his judgment. Part of the argument for the others against the people of this saying was that Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr informed in this hadith of his that they used to inherit by contracting to the exclusion of lineages. So Allah Almighty sent down, repelling that: Kin are nearer to one another in Allah's Book. So in this verse was the repelling of inheritance by contracting, and its obligation for kin to their exclusion. It did not clarify to us in this verse whether kin are the residuaries or others. It may be they are the residuaries, and it may be every kin, as came in the detail of inheritances in other than this hadith. Since what we mentioned was so, it is established that neither party has proof in this hadith; this hadith is only proof against one who would go to the inheritance of contractors, some from others, nothing but that. This is the meaning of Ibn al-Zubayr's hadith. The people of Badr went to inheritances of kin. Of what was narrated from them therein is what we mentioned earlier in this book, from Umar in his letter to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. Abu Ubaydah did not disapprove that against him - indicating his doctrine therein was like his. And:"
"7000","7438 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind informed me, from al-Shabi who said: Ziyad was approached concerning a man who died leaving his paternal aunt and maternal aunt. He said: Do you know how Umar judged therein? They said: No. He said: By Allah, I am the most knowledgeable of people of Umar's judgment therein: he made the paternal aunt in the place of the brother, and the maternal aunt in the place of the sister - so he gave the paternal aunt two-thirds, and the maternal aunt a third."
"7001","7439 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim and al-Mubarak ibn Fadalah informed me, from al-Hasan, from Umar, that he gave the paternal aunt two-thirds, and the maternal aunt a third."
"7002","7440 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Masruq who said: Abdillah was approached concerning maternal brothers and a mother: he gave the maternal brothers a third, and gave the mother the rest of the wealth, and said: The mother is the residuary of who has no residuary. He would not return upon maternal brothers with a mother, nor upon a son's daughter with a loin daughter, nor upon paternal sisters with a full sister, anything."
"7003","7441 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi informed me, from Abu Husayn, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from Masruq, from Abdillah who said: The maternal aunt is a mother."
"7004","7442 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Habib ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, from Amr ibn Harim, from Jabir ibn Zayd, that Umar judged for the paternal aunt two-thirds, and for the maternal aunt a third."
"7005","7443 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Bakr, from Abdillah, from Umar, the like of it."
"7006","7444 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, from Mansur, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim who said: Umar and Abdillah used to bequeath kin, to the exclusion of wala. I said: Did Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, do that? He said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was the strictest of them in that."
"7007","7445 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ubaydah informed me, from Hayyan al-Jufi, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, that a man died leaving a daughter, a wife and a freedwoman. Suwayd said: I was sitting with Ali, when there came to him the like of this case: he gave his daughter half, his wife an eighth, then returned the rest upon his daughter, and gave the freedman nothing."
"7008","7446 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Hayyan al-Jufi who said: He was with Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, and he mentioned the like of it."
"7009","7447 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Sharik informed me, from Jabir, from Abu Jafar who said: Ali used to return the remainder of inheritances upon those of shares, of kin."
"7010","7448 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi who said: Ziyad was approached concerning a maternal uncle and maternal aunt. He said: Shall I not inform you of Umar's judgment therein? He gave the maternal uncle two-thirds and gave the maternal aunt a third."
"7011","7449 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Shubah informed me, from Sulayman who said: Abdillah ibn Masud said: For the paternal aunt two-thirds, and for the maternal aunt a third. I said: Did you hear it from Ibrahim? He said: It is the most indicative I heard it from him."
"7012","7450 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Shubah, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Abdillah, the like of it. So here these people of Badr bequeathed kin by their kinship, though they were not residuaries. If to imitation, imitating these is worthier; if to what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we mentioned what was narrated from him in this chapter. If to examination: we saw residuaries inherit when male, and saw some of them, when having of nearness what some lack, thereby nearer to inheritance than farther. Muslims, when the dead has no residuary, all inherit him. When some are nearer to him than some, examination upon what we mentioned is that near to him be worthier of inheritance than farther to him of the deceased Muslims. Thereby what we mentioned is also established by examination - and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy upon them. We mentioned in these reports we narrated, from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), disagreement among them in some, after their agreeing upon inheritance by kinship not making its people residuaries. Of what they differed therein of inheritance of kin to the exclusion of freedmen - we mentioned that, from Umar, Ali and Abdillah. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the contrary of that."
"7013","7451 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Aban ibn Taghlib informed me, from al-Hakam, from Abdillah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had, that the daughter of Hamzah freed a slave of hers, and the slave died leaving her and leaving his daughter. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her half, and gave Hamzah's daughter half."
"7014","7452 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me, from al-Hakam who said: I heard Abdillah ibn Shaddad saying: She is my sister. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"7015","7453 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, who said: I came to Abdillah ibn Shaddad while he narrated to the people, saying: She is my sister. I asked them and they said: He was a slave of Hamzah's daughter. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"7016","7454 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Mansur ibn Hayyan al-Asadi, from Abdillah ibn Shaddad, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"7017","7455 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Yaqub and Abu Fazarah, who said: Abdillah ibn Shaddad narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it. Then he said: Do you know what is between me and her? She is my maternal sister; our mother was Asma bint Umays al-Khathamiyyah. So here the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bequeathed Hamzah's daughter, from her freedman, what remains after her daughter's share, by Allah Almighty's obligation for her - and did not return the rest upon the daughter. So these reports indicated that the freedman's master is worthier of inheritance than kin that is not a residuary. The like thereof has also been narrated from Ali."
"7018","7456 - Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, from Fitr, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah who said: Ali judged among people of us concerning one who left his daughter and his freedwoman: he gave his daughter half, and the freedwoman half."
"7019","7457 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed me, he said: Sufyan informed me, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl who said: I saw the woman whom Ali bequeathed from her father half, and bequeathed her freedman half. And this is examination also in our view: we saw the freedman, when with him no daughter, inherits by agnation, as the residuary of kin inherits. Examination upon that is that he be likewise, when with him a daughter, inheriting with her, as the residuary of kin inherits. This is the examination in this - and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy upon them. As for what we also mentioned from Abdillah, that he would not return upon maternal brothers with a mother anything, nor upon a son's daughter with a loin daughter, nor upon paternal sisters with full sisters, anything. We mentioned from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, the contrary of that: he would return the remainder of inheritances upon those of shares, of kin. Examination in our view therein is what Ali held, because they are all kin. We saw them in their obligations Allah Almighty obligated for them: they all inherited them by different kinship. Some were not, by nearness of kin, worthier of inheritance than others of them, of far kin. Examination upon that is that they all be, in what is returned upon them, of surpluses of inheritances, likewise - and near in kin not be preferred over farther in kin to the dead. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy upon them. There has been narrated from Ibrahim, in what we mentioned, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his giving Hamzah's daughter half, and her freedman's daughter half, that that was only a grant from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Hamzah's daughter."
"7020","7458 - Fahd narrated to us thereby, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim. And this, in our view, is corrupt speech: if all her father's inheritance became due to the daughter of Hamzah's daughter's freedman, by her kin to him, it is impossible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feed Hamzah's daughter with it. If all of that did not become due to her, but only half became due to her, what remains after that half returns to who freed him - namely Hamzah's daughter. So what Ibrahim mentioned therein became impossible, and it is established that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed to Hamzah's daughter was by inheritance, not by other than it. If one says: There have also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reports in bequeathing who is neither residuary nor kin. He mentioned what."
"7021","7459 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed me, from Amr ibn Dinar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard Awsajah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, narrating from Ibn Abbas, that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), leaving no kin except a slave he freed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him his inheritance. He said: So here the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bequeathed the lower freedman from the upper freedman - and you do not say this. It was said to him: There is not in this hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The lower freedman inherits the upper freedman. Therein is only that he handed his inheritance - his estate - to him, and it is not as narrated from him concerning the maternal uncle, that he said: He is heir of who has no heir. So it may bear aspects. Of them: his handing it to him because he bequeathed it to him by the dead's wealth upon him of wala. It may be his freedman was his kin, and he handed him his wealth by kin, and bequeathed it to him - not by wala. Do you not see him saying in the hadith: Leaving no kin except a slave he freed. So he informed that the slave was his kin, and bequeathed him by kinship. It may be he handed him his inheritance because the dead had commanded that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his wealth where he commanded it be placed - as narrated from Abdillah ibn Masud. For he."
"7022","7460 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Shabi, from Amr ibn Shurahbil who said: Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: There is no tribe of the Arabs worthier that a man of them die with no heir known to him, than you, O Hamdan; when so, let him place his wealth where he loves. Al-Amash said: I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: Hammam ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, may Allah be pleased with him, from Abdillah, the like of it."
"7023","7461 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Ibn Masud, the like of it."
"7024","7462 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Abdillah, the like of it."
"7025","7463 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, who said: I heard Abu Amr al-Shaybani narrating from Ibn Masud who said: The saibah places his wealth where he loves."
"7026","7464 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr and Abu al-Walid narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Abdillah, the like of it."
"7027","7465 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Abdillah, the like of it. It may be the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed the lower freedman due to his poverty, as the imam may do that, in what is in his hand of wealth with no owner. I heard Ibn Abi Imran mentioning that this other interpretation has been narrated from Yahya ibn Adam. Since this hadith bore what we mentioned, none may carry it upon one interpretation thereof, except with a proof indicating him upon it - from Allah's Book, His Messenger's sunnah, or consensus. There has been narrated in the like thereof what."
"7028","7466 - Yunus and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Ahmar, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father who said: A man of Khuzaah died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought his inheritance, and he said: Seek for him an heir or kin. Thus Yunus said. And Ibn Khuzaymah said: Or kin. They sought and found none. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hand to the eldest of Khuzaah. This, in our view - and Allah knows best - is upon what Yahya ibn Adam said before this. And:"
"7029","7467 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani, from Mujahid, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that a freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell from a palm-tree and died. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: See, has he an heir? They said: No. He said: Give his wealth to some kin. So it may be the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended thereby his kin - the dead's kin - intending to make it a bond from him to them. And Allah knows best. The book is complete, with the aid of Allah, the Bestower."
